window.LSD_REGISTRY = {
"optim-problem": {
"p": "01_convex_analysis.html",
"k": "def",
"t": "Minimization problem, Argmin, and the $+\\infty$ convention",
"s": "<p>Let $f:D\\to[-\\infty,+\\infty]$ be a <em>cost function</em> on a set $D\\subset\\R^N$. A minimization problem is\n$$\\text{Find }\\hat x\\in D\\ \\text{ such that }\\ (\\forall x\\in D)\\ f(\\hat x)\\le f(x)\\ \\iff\\ f(\\hat x)=\\inf_{x\\in D}f(x)\\ \\iff\\ \\hat x\\in\\Argmin_{x\\in D} f(x).$$\nA maximization problem ($f$ a <em>reward</em>) is the same thing for $-f$: $\\ (\\forall x\\in D)\\ f(\\hat x)\\ge f(x)\\iff \\hat x\\in\\Argmin_{x\\in D}\\big(-f(x)\\big)$. Without loss of generality we study minimization problems with $f:D\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$.</p>\n<p><strong>Types.</strong> $D=\\R^N$: unconstrained. $D$ countable: discrete ($D$ finite: combinatorial; $D\\subset\\Z^N$: integer). $D$ uncountable: continuous. With $P$ equality and $Q$ inequality constraints,\n$$D=\\{x\\in\\R^N\\mid (\\forall i\\in\\{1,\\dots,P\\})\\ \\varphi_i(x)=\\delta_i\\ \\text{ and }\\ (\\forall j\\in\\{1,\\dots,Q\\})\\ \\psi_j(x)\\le\\eta_j\\},$$\nwith $\\delta_i,\\eta_j\\in\\R$ and $\\varphi_i,\\psi_j:\\R^N\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$. If $\\varphi_i=\\ps{\\cdot}{u_i}$ (resp. $\\psi_j=\\ps{\\cdot}{u_j}$) the constraint is linear (affine); if in addition $f$ is linear, the problem is a <em>linear program</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Remark ($+\\infty$ convention).</strong> $\\displaystyle \\hat x\\in\\Argmin_{x\\in D}f(x)\\iff \\hat x\\in\\Argmin_{x\\in\\R^N}\\tilde f(x)$, where $\\tilde f(x)=f(x)$ if $x\\in D$ and $\\tilde f(x)=+\\infty$ otherwise. Allowing non-finite values gives a unifying view of constrained and unconstrained problems.</p>"
},
"main-questions": {
"p": "01_convex_analysis.html",
"k": "rem",
"t": "The four questions: existence, characterization, algorithm, performance",
"s": "<ol>\n<li><strong>Existence / uniqueness</strong> of a solution $\\hat x$?</li>\n<li><strong>Characterization</strong> of solutions: necessary / sufficient conditions for $\\hat x$ to be a solution.</li>\n<li><strong>Designing an algorithm</strong> to approximate a solution in the frequent case when no closed form is available, i.e. building a sequence $(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ of $\\R^N$ such that $\\lim_{n\\to+\\infty}x_n=\\hat x$.</li>\n<li><strong>Evaluation of the performance</strong> of the algorithm: convergence speed (<a class=\"xref\" data-id=\"convergence-rates\" href=\"#convergence-rates\">rates</a>), robustness to numerical errors, amenability to parallel / distributed implementations.</li>\n</ol>"
},
"convergence-rates": {
"p": "01_convex_analysis.html",
"k": "def",
"t": "Q-linear and Q-superlinear convergence",
"s": "<p>Let $(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ converge to $\\hat x$. If there exist $\\rho\\in\\,]0,1[$ and $n^*\\in\\N$ such that\n$$(\\forall n\\ge n^*)\\qquad \\norm{x_{n+1}-\\hat x}\\le\\rho\\,\\norm{x_n-\\hat x},$$\nthe convergence rate is <strong>Q-linear</strong>. If $\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\to+\\infty}\\frac{\\norm{x_{n+1}-\\hat x}}{\\norm{x_n-\\hat x}}=0$, the rate is <strong>Q-superlinear</strong>.</p>"
},
"examples-applications": {
"p": "01_convex_analysis.html",
"k": "rem",
"t": "Running examples: mesh denoising, segmentation, consensus, logistic regression",
"s": "<p><strong>Valued graph.</strong> $\\mathcal V=\\{v^{(i)}\\mid i\\in\\{1,\\dots,M\\}\\}$ is the set of vertices (objects), $\\mathcal E=\\{e^{(i,j)}\\mid (i,j)\\in\\mathbb E\\}$ the set of edges (object relationships). A directed reflexive graph has $|\\mathbb E|\\le M^2$ edges, a directed nonreflexive one $|\\mathbb E|\\le M(M-1)$, an undirected nonreflexive one $|\\mathbb E|\\le M(M-1)/2$. Weights $(x^{(i)})_{1\\le i\\le M}$ live on vertices and $(x^{(i,j)})_{(i,j)\\in\\mathbb E}$ on edges (scalars or vectors).</p>\n<p><strong>Mesh denoising</strong> (undirected nonreflexive graph: vertices and edges of the mesh; $x^{(i)}\\in\\R^3$ the true coordinates of vertex $i$, $y^{(i)}$ the measured ones; $N=3M$, $D=\\R^N$):\n$$f(x)=\\sum_{i=1}^M\\Big(\\norm{x^{(i)}-y^{(i)}}^2+\\Big(\\sum_{j\\in\\mathbb N_i}\\omega_{i,j}\\norm{x^{(i)}-x^{(j)}}^2\\Big)^{1/2}\\Big),$$\n$\\mathbb N_i$ the neighbourhood of vertex $i$, $(\\omega_{i,j})_{j\\in\\mathbb N_i}$ nonnegative regularization constants.</p>\n<p><strong>Segmentation</strong> (undirected nonreflexive regular graph of pixels; $x^{(i)}\\in\\mathbb L$ the label of pixel $i$, $\\mathbb L\\subset\\R$ finite; $y^{(i)}$ its intensity; $N=M$, $D=\\mathbb L^M$):\n$$f(x)=\\sum_{i=1}^M\\alpha_i^p\\,\\abs{x^{(i)}-y^{(i)}}^p+\\sum_{(i,j)\\in\\mathbb E}\\omega_{i,j}^q\\,\\abs{x^{(i)}-x^{(j)}}^q,\\qquad (p,q)\\in[1,+\\infty[^2,$$\n$(\\alpha_i)_i$, $(\\omega_{i,j})_{(i,j)}$ nonnegative constants.</p><p class=\"mini\">…</p>"
},
"hilbert-space": {
"p": "01_convex_analysis.html",
"k": "def",
"t": "Hilbert space, bounded linear operators, adjoint",
"s": "<p>A (real) <strong>Hilbert space</strong> $\\H$ is a complete real vector space endowed with an inner product $\\ps{\\cdot}{\\cdot}$. The associated norm is $(\\forall x\\in\\H)\\ \\norm{x}=\\sqrt{\\ps{x}{x}}$. Particular case: $\\H=\\R^N$ (Euclidean space of dimension $N$). $2^{\\H}$ denotes the power set of $\\H$ (the family of all subsets of $\\H$).</p>\n<p>Let $\\H$ and $\\G$ be two Hilbert spaces. A linear operator $L:\\H\\to\\G$ is <strong>bounded</strong> (or continuous) if\n$$\\norm{L}=\\sup_{\\norm{x}\\le1}\\norm{Lx}\\lt+\\infty.$$\nIn finite dimension, every linear operator is bounded. $\\B(\\H,\\G)$ is the Banach space of bounded linear operators from $\\H$ to $\\G$.</p>\n<p>Let $L\\in\\B(\\H,\\G)$. Its <strong>adjoint</strong> $L^*$ is the operator in $\\B(\\G,\\H)$ defined by\n$$(\\forall(x,y)\\in\\H\\times\\G)\\qquad \\ps{Lx}{y}=\\ps{x}{L^*y}.$$\nWe have $\\norm{L^*}=\\norm{L}$. If $L$ is bijective (an <em>isomorphism</em>), then $L^{-1}\\in\\B(\\G,\\H)$ and $(L^{-1})^*=(L^*)^{-1}$. If $\\H=\\R^N$ and $\\G=\\R^M$, then $L^*=L^\\top$.</p>"
},
"adjoint-example": {
"p": "01_convex_analysis.html",
"k": "prop",
"t": "The adjoint of $x\\mapsto(x,\\dots,x)$ is the sum",
"s": "<p>Let $n\\ge1$ and let $\\H^n$ carry the inner product $\\ps{x}{y}=\\sum_{i=1}^n\\ps{x_i}{y_i}$. If\n$$L:\\H\\to\\H^n:\\ x\\mapsto(x,\\dots,x),\\qquad\\text{then}\\qquad L^*:\\H^n\\to\\H:\\ y=(y_1,\\dots,y_n)\\mapsto\\sum_{i=1}^n y_i.$$</p>"
},
"domain-proper": {
"p": "01_convex_analysis.html",
"k": "def",
"t": "Domain and proper functions",
"s": "<p>Let $f:\\H\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$ where $\\H$ is a Hilbert space.</p>\n<ul><li>The <strong>domain</strong> of $f$ is $\\dom f=\\{x\\in\\H\\mid f(x)\\lt+\\infty\\}$.</li>\n<li>The function $f$ is <strong>proper</strong> if $\\dom f\\neq\\emptyset$.</li></ul>"
},
"indicator-function": {
"p": "01_convex_analysis.html",
"k": "def",
"t": "Indicator function of a set",
"s": "<p>Let $C\\subset\\H$. The indicator function of $C$ is\n$$(\\forall x\\in\\H)\\qquad \\ind_C(x)=\\begin{cases}0 &amp; \\text{if } x\\in C\\\\ +\\infty &amp; \\text{otherwise.}\\end{cases}$$\nExample: $C=[\\delta_1,\\delta_2]$.</p>"
},
"epigraph": {
"p": "01_convex_analysis.html",
"k": "def",
"t": "Epigraph",
"s": "<p>Let $f:\\H\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$. The epigraph of $f$ is\n$$\\epi f=\\{(x,\\zeta)\\in\\dom f\\times\\R\\mid f(x)\\le\\zeta\\}\\subset\\H\\times\\R.$$</p>"
},
"lsc": {
"p": "01_convex_analysis.html",
"k": "def",
"t": "Lower semicontinuity: the sequential definition",
"s": "<p>Let $f:\\H\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$. $f$ is <strong>lower semi-continuous (l.s.c.) at $x\\in\\H$</strong> if, for every sequence $(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ of $\\H$,\n$$x_n\\to x\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\liminf_{n\\to+\\infty} f(x_n)\\ \\ge\\ f(x).$$\n$f$ is lower semi-continuous on $\\H$ if it is l.s.c. at every $x\\in\\H$. Equivalently (<a class=\"xref\" data-id=\"lsc-epi-closed\" href=\"#lsc-epi-closed\">next card</a>): $f$ is l.s.c. on $\\H$ if and only if $\\epi f$ is closed.</p>"
},
"lsc-epi-closed": {
"p": "01_convex_analysis.html",
"k": "prop",
"t": "$f$ is l.s.c. on $\\H$ $\\iff$ $\\epi f$ is closed",
"s": "<p>Let $f:\\H\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$. Then $f$ is lower semi-continuous on $\\H$ if and only if $\\epi f$ is a closed subset of $\\H\\times\\R$.</p>"
},
"lsc-stability": {
"p": "01_convex_analysis.html",
"k": "prop",
"t": "Continuous ⇒ l.s.c.; finite sums and suprema of l.s.c. functions are l.s.c.",
"s": "<ul><li>Every continuous function on $\\H$ is l.s.c.</li>\n<li>Every finite sum of l.s.c. functions is l.s.c.</li>\n<li>Let $(f_i)_{i\\in I}$ be a family of l.s.c. functions. Then $\\sup_{i\\in I}f_i$ is l.s.c.</li></ul>"
},
"convergence-strong-weak": {
"p": "01_convex_analysis.html",
"k": "def",
"t": "Strong and weak convergence",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$ be a Hilbert space, $(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ a sequence in $\\H$ and $\\hat x\\in\\H$.</p>\n<ul><li>$(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ <strong>converges strongly</strong> to $\\hat x$ if $\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\to+\\infty}\\norm{x_n-\\hat x}=0$. Notation: $x_n\\to\\hat x$.</li>\n<li>$(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ <strong>converges weakly</strong> to $\\hat x$ if $\\displaystyle(\\forall y\\in\\H)\\ \\lim_{n\\to+\\infty}\\ps{y}{x_n-\\hat x}=0$. Notation: $x_n\\wto\\hat x$.</li></ul>\n<p><em>Remark:</em> $x_n\\to x\\ \\Rightarrow\\ x_n\\wto x$. In a finite-dimensional Hilbert space, strong and weak convergence are equivalent.</p>"
},
"bounded-closed-compact": {
"p": "01_convex_analysis.html",
"k": "def",
"t": "Bounded, closed and compact sets",
"s": "<p>Let $S$ be a subset of a Hilbert space $\\H$.</p>\n<ul><li>$S$ is <strong>bounded</strong> if it is included in a ball.</li>\n<li>$S$ is <strong>closed</strong> if the limit of every converging sequence of elements of $S$ belongs to $S$.</li>\n<li>$S$ is <strong>compact</strong> if, from every sequence $(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ of $S$, one can extract a subsequence $(x_{n_k})_{k\\in\\N}$ which converges to a point of $S$.</li></ul>\n<p>If $S$ is compact, then it is closed and bounded. The converse holds when $\\H$ is finite-dimensional.</p>"
},
"liminf-limsup": {
"p": "01_convex_analysis.html",
"k": "def",
"t": "Limits inferior and superior",
"s": "<p>Let $(\\xi_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ be a sequence of elements of $[-\\infty,+\\infty]$. Its <strong>infimum limit</strong> is\n$$\\liminf_{n\\to+\\infty}\\xi_n=\\lim_{n\\to+\\infty}\\inf\\{\\xi_k\\mid k\\ge n\\}\\in[-\\infty,+\\infty]$$\nand its <strong>supremum limit</strong> is $\\displaystyle\\limsup_{n\\to+\\infty}\\xi_n=\\lim_{n\\to+\\infty}\\sup\\{\\xi_k\\mid k\\ge n\\}\\in[-\\infty,+\\infty]$.</p>\n<ul><li>$\\limsup\\xi_n=-\\liminf(-\\xi_n)$;</li>\n<li>$\\liminf\\xi_n\\le\\limsup\\xi_n$;</li>\n<li>$\\lim_{n\\to+\\infty}\\xi_n=\\xi\\in[-\\infty,+\\infty]$ if and only if $\\liminf\\xi_n=\\limsup\\xi_n=\\xi$.</li></ul>"
},
"minimizers": {
"p": "01_convex_analysis.html",
"k": "def",
"t": "Local, global and strict minimizers",
"s": "<p>Let $S$ be a nonempty set of a Hilbert space $\\H$, let $f:S\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$ be a proper function and let $\\hat x\\in S$.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>$\\hat x$ is a <strong>local minimizer</strong> of $f$ if $\\hat x\\in\\dom f$ and there exists an open neighbourhood $O$ of $\\hat x$ such that $(\\forall x\\in O\\cap S)\\ f(\\hat x)\\le f(x)$.</li>\n<li>$\\hat x$ is a <strong>(global) minimizer</strong> of $f$ if $(\\forall x\\in S)\\ f(\\hat x)\\le f(x)$.</li>\n<li>$\\hat x$ is a <strong>strict local minimizer</strong> of $f$ if there exists an open neighbourhood $O$ of $\\hat x$ such that $(\\forall x\\in(O\\cap S)\\setminus\\{\\hat x\\})\\ f(\\hat x)\\lt f(x)$.</li>\n<li>$\\hat x$ is a <strong>strict (global) minimizer</strong> of $f$ if $(\\forall x\\in S\\setminus\\{\\hat x\\})\\ f(\\hat x)\\lt f(x)$.</li>\n</ul>"
},
"weierstrass": {
"p": "01_convex_analysis.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Weierstrass: l.s.c. on a compact set ⇒ a minimizer exists",
"s": "<p>Let $S$ be a nonempty compact set of a Hilbert space $\\H$. Let $f:S\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$ be a proper l.s.c. function. Then there exists $\\hat x\\in S$ such that\n$$f(\\hat x)=\\inf_{x\\in S}f(x).$$</p>"
},
"coercive": {
"p": "01_convex_analysis.html",
"k": "def",
"t": "Coercive function",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$ be a Hilbert space and $f:\\H\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$. $f$ is <strong>coercive</strong> if\n$$\\lim_{\\norm x\\to+\\infty}f(x)=+\\infty,$$\ni.e. for every $M\\in\\R$ there exists $R\\gt0$ such that $\\norm x\\gt R\\Rightarrow f(x)\\gt M$.</p>"
},
"existence-coercive": {
"p": "01_convex_analysis.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Existence in finite dimension: proper + l.s.c. + coercive ⇒ Argmin nonempty compact",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$ be a finite-dimensional Hilbert space. Let $f:\\H\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$ be a proper l.s.c. coercive function. Then the set of minimizers of $f$ is a nonempty compact set.</p>"
},
"convex-set": {
"p": "01_convex_analysis.html",
"k": "def",
"t": "Convex set",
"s": "<p>$C\\subset\\H$ is a <strong>convex set</strong> if\n$$(\\forall(x,y)\\in C^2)(\\forall\\alpha\\in\\,]0,1[)\\qquad \\alpha x+(1-\\alpha)y\\in C .$$</p>"
},
"gateaux": {
"p": "01_convex_analysis.html",
"k": "def",
"t": "Gâteaux differentiability",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$ be a Hilbert space and let $f:\\H\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$ be a proper function. $f$ is <strong>Gâteaux differentiable</strong> at $x\\in\\dom f$ if there exists $\\nabla f(x)\\in\\H$ such that\n$$(\\forall y\\in\\H)\\qquad \\ps{\\nabla f(x)}{y}=\\lim_{\\substack{\\alpha\\to0\\\\ \\alpha\\neq0}}\\frac{f(x+\\alpha y)-f(x)}{\\alpha}.$$</p>"
},
"optimality-convex-set": {
"p": "01_convex_analysis.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "First-order optimality over a convex set (variational inequality)",
"s": "<p>Let $C$ be a nonempty convex subset of a Hilbert space $\\H$. Let $f:\\H\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$ be Gâteaux differentiable at $\\hat x\\in C$. If $\\hat x$ is a local minimizer of $f$ over $C$, then\n$$(\\forall y\\in C)\\qquad \\ps{\\nabla f(\\hat x)}{y-\\hat x}\\ \\ge\\ 0 .$$\nIf $\\hat x\\in\\inte(C)$, the condition reduces to $\\nabla f(\\hat x)=0$.</p>"
},
"convex-function": {
"p": "01_convex_analysis.html",
"k": "def",
"t": "Convex function, concave function",
"s": "<p>$f:\\H\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$ is a <strong>convex function</strong> if\n$$(\\forall(x,y)\\in\\H^2)(\\forall\\alpha\\in\\,]0,1[)\\qquad f(\\alpha x+(1-\\alpha)y)\\ \\le\\ \\alpha f(x)+(1-\\alpha)f(y),$$\nwith the conventions $a+(+\\infty)=+\\infty$ and $\\alpha\\cdot(+\\infty)=+\\infty$ for $\\alpha\\gt0$.</p>\n<p>$f:\\H\\to[-\\infty,+\\infty[$ is <strong>concave</strong> if $-f$ is convex.</p>"
},
"convex-epi": {
"p": "01_convex_analysis.html",
"k": "prop",
"t": "$f$ is convex $\\iff$ $\\epi f$ is convex; the domain of a convex function is convex",
"s": "<p>Let $f:\\H\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$. Then $f$ is convex if and only if its epigraph $\\epi f$ is a convex subset of $\\H\\times\\R$. Consequently, if $f$ is convex then $\\dom f$ is convex.</p>"
},
"subdifferential": {
"p": "02_subdifferential_conjugate.html",
"k": "def",
"t": "The (Moreau) subdifferential",
"s": "<p>Let $f:\\H\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$ be a proper function. The (Moreau) subdifferential of $f$, denoted by $\\partial f$, is the set-valued map\n$$\\partial f:\\H\\to2^{\\H},\\qquad x\\mapsto\\big\\{u\\in\\H\\ \\big|\\ (\\forall y\\in\\H)\\ \\ \\ps{y-x}{u}+f(x)\\le f(y)\\big\\}.$$\nAn element $u\\in\\partial f(x)$ is called a <em>subgradient</em> of $f$ at $x$.</p>"
},
"fermat-rule": {
"p": "02_subdifferential_conjugate.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Fermat's rule: $0\\in\\partial f(x)\\iff x\\in\\Argmin f$",
"s": "<p>Let $f:\\H\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$ be proper. For every $x\\in\\H$,\n$$0\\in\\partial f(x)\\iff x\\in\\Argmin f.$$</p>"
},
"subdifferential-properties": {
"p": "02_subdifferential_conjugate.html",
"k": "prop",
"t": "Subgradients; $\\partial f(x)=\\emptyset$ outside the domain; $\\partial f(x)$ is closed and convex",
"s": "<p>Let $f:\\H\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$ be proper.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>$u\\in\\partial f(x)$ is called a subgradient of $f$ at $x$.</li>\n<li>If $x\\notin\\dom f$, then $\\partial f(x)=\\emptyset$.</li>\n<li>For every $x\\in\\dom f$, $\\partial f(x)$ is a closed and convex set.</li>\n</ul>"
},
"subdifferential-monotone": {
"p": "02_subdifferential_conjugate.html",
"k": "prop",
"t": "The subdifferential is a monotone operator",
"s": "<p>Let $f:\\H\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$ be proper. Its subdifferential is a monotone operator, i.e.\n$$\\big(\\forall(x_1,x_2)\\in\\H^2\\big)\\big(\\forall u_1\\in\\partial f(x_1)\\big)\\big(\\forall u_2\\in\\partial f(x_2)\\big)\\qquad\\ps{u_1-u_2}{x_1-x_2}\\ge0.$$</p>"
},
"subdiff-gateaux": {
"p": "02_subdifferential_conjugate.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Convex and Gâteaux differentiable at $x$ $\\Rightarrow$ $\\partial f(x)=\\{\\nabla f(x)\\}$",
"s": "<p>If $f:\\H\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$ is convex and Gâteaux differentiable at $x$, then\n$$\\partial f(x)=\\{\\nabla f(x)\\},\\qquad\\text{where}\\quad(\\forall y\\in\\H)\\quad\\ps{\\nabla f(x)}{y}=\\lim_{\\alpha\\to0,\\ \\alpha\\ne0}\\frac{f(x+\\alpha y)-f(x)}{\\alpha}.$$</p>"
},
"gradient-inequality": {
"p": "02_subdifferential_conjugate.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Convexity $\\iff$ gradient inequality",
"s": "<p>Let $f:\\H\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$ be Gâteaux differentiable on $\\dom f$, which is an open convex set. Then $f$ is convex if and only if\n$$\\big(\\forall(x,y)\\in(\\dom f)^2\\big)\\qquad f(y)\\ge f(x)+\\ps{\\nabla f(x)}{y-x}.$$</p>"
},
"gradient-monotone": {
"p": "02_subdifferential_conjugate.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Convexity $\\iff$ monotone gradient",
"s": "<p>Let $f:\\H\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$ be Gâteaux differentiable on $\\dom f$, which is an open convex set. Then $f$ is convex if and only if $\\nabla f$ is monotone on $\\dom f$, i.e.\n$$\\big(\\forall(x,y)\\in(\\dom f)^2\\big)\\qquad\\ps{\\nabla f(y)-\\nabla f(x)}{y-x}\\ge0.$$</p>"
},
"normal-cone": {
"p": "02_subdifferential_conjugate.html",
"k": "prop",
"t": "$\\partial\\ind_C$ is the normal cone; interior, subspace and ball examples",
"s": "<p>Let $C$ be a nonempty subset of $\\H$. For every $x\\in\\H$, $\\partial\\ind_C(x)$ is the <em>normal cone</em> to $C$ at $x$, defined by\n$$N_C(x)=\\begin{cases}\\big\\{u\\in\\H\\ \\big|\\ (\\forall y\\in C)\\ \\ps{u}{y-x}\\le0\\big\\}&amp;\\text{if }x\\in C,\\\\ \\emptyset&amp;\\text{otherwise.}\\end{cases}$$</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If $x\\in\\inte C$, then $N_C(x)=\\{0\\}$.</li>\n<li>If $C$ is a vector space, then for every $x\\in C$, $N_C(x)=C^\\perp$.</li>\n<li>Let $c\\in\\H$, $\\rho\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$ and $C=\\overline{B}(c,\\rho)=\\{y\\in\\H\\ |\\ \\norm{y-c}\\le\\rho\\}$. For every $x\\in C$,\n$$N_C(x)=\\begin{cases}\\{\\alpha(x-c)\\ |\\ \\alpha\\in[0,+\\infty[\\}&amp;\\text{if }\\norm{x-c}=\\rho,\\\\ \\{0\\}&amp;\\text{if }\\norm{x-c}\\lt\\rho.\\end{cases}$$</li>\n</ul>"
},
"subdiff-scaling": {
"p": "02_subdifferential_conjugate.html",
"k": "prop",
"t": "$\\partial(\\lambda f)=\\lambda\\,\\partial f$ for $\\lambda\\gt0$",
"s": "<p>Let $f:\\H\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$ be proper. Then for every $\\lambda\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$, $\\partial(\\lambda f)=\\lambda\\,\\partial f$, i.e. $\\partial(\\lambda f)(x)=\\{\\lambda u\\ |\\ u\\in\\partial f(x)\\}$ for every $x$.</p>"
},
"subdiff-sum-inclusion": {
"p": "02_subdifferential_conjugate.html",
"k": "prop",
"t": "$\\partial f(x)+L^*\\partial g(Lx)\\subset\\partial(f+g\\circ L)(x)$",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$ and $\\G$ be two real Hilbert spaces, $f:\\H\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$, $g:\\G\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$ and $L\\in\\B(\\H,\\G)$. If $\\dom g\\cap L(\\dom f)\\ne\\emptyset$, then\n$$(\\forall x\\in\\H)\\qquad\\partial f(x)+L^*\\partial g(Lx)\\subset\\partial(f+g\\circ L)(x).$$</p>"
},
"moreau-rockafellar": {
"p": "02_subdifferential_conjugate.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Sum rule with equality under a qualification condition (Moreau–Rockafellar)",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$ and $\\G$ be two real Hilbert spaces, $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$, $g\\in\\Gz{\\G}$ and $L\\in\\B(\\H,\\G)$. If\n$$\\inte(\\dom g)\\cap L(\\dom f)\\ne\\emptyset\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad\\dom g\\cap\\inte\\big(L(\\dom f)\\big)\\ne\\emptyset,$$\nthen $$\\partial f+L^*\\partial g\\,L=\\partial(f+g\\circ L).$$\nParticular cases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$, $g\\in\\Gz{\\H}$ and $g$ is finite valued, then $\\partial f+\\partial g=\\partial(f+g)$.</li>\n<li>If $g\\in\\Gz{\\G}$, $L\\in\\B(\\H,\\G)$ and $\\inte(\\dom g)\\cap\\ran L\\ne\\emptyset$, then $L^*\\partial g\\,L=\\partial(g\\circ L)$.</li>\n</ul>"
},
"subdiff-separable": {
"p": "02_subdifferential_conjugate.html",
"k": "prop",
"t": "Subdifferential of a separable sum: $\\partial f(x)=\\times_i\\,\\partial f_i(x_i)$",
"s": "<p>Let $(\\H_i)_{i\\in I}$, $I\\subset\\N$, be Hilbert spaces and $\\H=\\bigoplus_{i\\in I}\\H_i$. For every $i\\in I$ let $f_i:\\H_i\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$ be proper, and set\n$$f:\\H\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]:\\ x=(x_i)_{i\\in I}\\mapsto\\sum_{i\\in I}f_i(x_i).$$\nThen $$\\big(\\forall x=(x_i)_{i\\in I}\\in\\H\\big)\\qquad\\partial f(x)=\\underset{i\\in I}{\\times}\\,\\partial f_i(x_i).$$</p>"
},
"ex-huber": {
"p": "02_subdifferential_conjugate.html",
"k": "ex",
"t": "The Huber function",
"s": "<p>Let $\\rho\\gt0$ and set\n$$f:\\R\\to\\R:\\ x\\mapsto\\begin{cases}\\dfrac{x^2}{2}&amp;\\text{if }\\abs{x}\\le\\rho,\\\\[4pt]\\rho\\abs{x}-\\dfrac{\\rho^2}{2}&amp;\\text{otherwise.}\\end{cases}$$</p>\n<ol><li>What is the domain of $f$?</li><li>Is $f$ differentiable? twice-differentiable?</li><li>Prove that $f$ is convex. Is it strictly convex?</li></ol>"
},
"ex-constrained-minimizer": {
"p": "02_subdifferential_conjugate.html",
"k": "ex",
"t": "Minimizers of $f+\\ind_C$",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$ be a Hilbert space. Let $f:\\H\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$ and let $C\\subset\\H$ such that $\\dom f\\cap C\\ne\\emptyset$.</p>\n<ol><li>Give a sufficient condition for $x\\in\\H$ to be a global minimizer of $f+\\ind_C$.</li><li>When $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$, $C$ is a closed convex set, and $f$ is Gâteaux-differentiable on $\\H$, give a necessary and sufficient condition for $x\\in\\H$ to be a minimizer of $f+\\ind_C$.</li></ol>"
},
"conjugate": {
"p": "02_subdifferential_conjugate.html",
"k": "def",
"t": "The Legendre–Fenchel conjugate",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$ be a Hilbert space and $f:\\H\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$. The conjugate of $f$ is the function $f^*:\\H\\to[-\\infty,+\\infty]$ such that\n$$(\\forall u\\in\\H)\\qquad f^*(u)=\\sup_{x\\in\\H}\\big(\\ps{x}{u}-f(x)\\big)=\\sup_{x\\in\\dom f}\\big(\\ps{x}{u}-f(x)\\big).$$</p>"
},
"conjugate-examples": {
"p": "02_subdifferential_conjugate.html",
"k": "prop",
"t": "Conjugates of $\\tfrac12\\norm{\\cdot}^2$, of radial functions, of $\\tfrac1q\\norm{\\cdot}_q^q$",
"s": "<ul>\n<li>$f=\\tfrac12\\norm{\\cdot}^2\\ \\Rightarrow\\ f^*=\\tfrac12\\norm{\\cdot}^2$.</li>\n<li>Let $\\varphi:\\R\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$ be an even function. Then $(\\varphi\\circ\\norm{\\cdot})^*=\\varphi^*\\circ\\norm{\\cdot}$.</li>\n<li>$(\\forall x\\in\\R^N)\\ f(x)=\\tfrac1q\\norm{x}_q^q$ with $q\\in\\,]1,+\\infty[$ $\\ \\Rightarrow\\ $ $(\\forall u\\in\\R^N)\\ f^*(u)=\\tfrac1{q^*}\\norm{u}_{q^*}^{q^*}$ with $\\tfrac1q+\\tfrac1{q^*}=1$.</li>\n</ul>"
},
"conjugate-properties": {
"p": "02_subdifferential_conjugate.html",
"k": "prop",
"t": "Conjugate calculus: parity, scaling, translation, isomorphism, lsc convexity",
"s": "<p>Let $f:\\H\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If $f$ is even, then $f^*$ is even.</li>\n<li>For every $\\alpha\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$, $(\\alpha f)^*=\\alpha f^*(\\cdot/\\alpha)$.</li>\n<li>For every $(y,v)\\in\\H^2$ and $\\alpha\\in\\R$, $\\big(f(\\cdot-y)+\\ps{\\cdot}{v}+\\alpha\\big)^*=f^*(\\cdot-v)+\\ps{y}{\\cdot-v}-\\alpha$.</li>\n<li>Let $\\G$ be a Hilbert space and $L\\in\\B(\\G,\\H)$ be an isomorphism. Then $(f\\circ L)^*=f^*\\circ(L^{-1})^*$.</li>\n<li>$f^*$ is lsc and convex.</li>\n</ul>"
},
"moreau-fenchel": {
"p": "02_subdifferential_conjugate.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Moreau–Fenchel: $f$ lsc convex $\\iff$ $f^{**}=f$",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$ be a Hilbert space and $f:\\H\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$ be a proper function. Then\n$$f\\text{ is lsc and convex}\\iff f^{**}=f.$$\n<strong>Consequence.</strong> If $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$, then $f^*$ is proper, hence $f^*\\in\\Gz{\\H}$.</p>"
},
"support-function": {
"p": "02_subdifferential_conjugate.html",
"k": "def",
"t": "Support function $\\sigma_C=\\ind_C^*$",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$ be a Hilbert space and $C\\subset\\H$. The support function of $C$ is\n$$(\\forall u\\in\\H)\\qquad\\sigma_C(u)=\\sup_{x\\in C}\\ps{x}{u}=\\ind_C^*(u).$$</p>"
},
"positively-homogeneous": {
"p": "02_subdifferential_conjugate.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Positively homogeneous $\\Gz{}$ functions are exactly the support functions",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$ be a Hilbert space. A function $f:\\H\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$ is <em>positively homogeneous</em> if\n$$(\\forall x\\in\\H)(\\forall\\alpha\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[)\\qquad f(\\alpha x)=\\alpha f(x).$$\nThen: $f$ is positively homogeneous and belongs to $\\Gz{\\H}$ if and only if $f=\\sigma_C$ where $C$ is a nonempty closed convex subset of $\\H$.</p>"
},
"support-examples": {
"p": "02_subdifferential_conjugate.html",
"k": "prop",
"t": "Piecewise linear functions, $\\ell^q$ norms and the $\\ell^1$ norm as support functions",
"s": "<ul>\n<li>Let $f:\\R\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]:\\ x\\mapsto\\begin{cases}\\delta_1x&amp;\\text{if }x\\lt0\\\\ 0&amp;\\text{if }x=0\\\\ \\delta_2x&amp;\\text{if }x\\gt0\\end{cases}$ with $-\\infty\\le\\delta_1\\lt\\delta_2\\le+\\infty$. Then $f=\\sigma_C$ where $C$ is the closed real interval such that $\\inf C=\\delta_1$ and $\\sup C=\\delta_2$.</li>\n<li>Let $f$ be an $\\ell^q$ norm of $\\R^N$ with $q\\in[1,+\\infty]$. Then $f=\\sigma_C$ where $C=\\{y\\in\\R^N\\ |\\ \\norm{y}_{q^*}\\le1\\}$ with $\\tfrac1q+\\tfrac1{q^*}=1$.</li>\n<li>Particular case: the $\\ell^1$ norm of $\\R^N$: $C=[-1,1]^N$.</li>\n</ul>"
},
"prox-motivation": {
"p": "03_proximity_operator.html",
"k": "rem",
"t": "Explicit versus implicit gradient steps",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$ be a real Hilbert space and $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$ with a $\\beta$-Lipschitz gradient. We look for $\\hat x\\in\\Argmin f$.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gradient descent</strong> (explicit): $\\gamma\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$, $x_0\\in\\H$, $x_{n+1}=x_n-\\gamma\\nabla f(x_n)$. The sequence converges to a minimizer of $f$ provided one exists and <span class=\"hl\">$\\gamma\\in\\,]0,2/\\beta[$</span>.</li>\n<li><strong>Alternative scheme</strong> (implicit): $x_{n+1}=x_n-\\gamma\\nabla f(x_{n+1})$ — the gradient is evaluated at the <em>arrival</em> point.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Three questions (slide 3): <em>How to determine $x_{n+1}$ at each iteration of the implicit scheme? Which values of $\\gamma$ guarantee convergence? What to do if $f$ is nonsmooth?</em></p>"
},
"moreau-envelope": {
"p": "03_proximity_operator.html",
"k": "def",
"t": "Moreau envelope $\\env{\\gamma}{f}$",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$ be a Hilbert space, $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$ and $\\gamma\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$. The <strong>Moreau envelope</strong> of $f$ of parameter $\\gamma$ is\n$$\\env{\\gamma}{f}:\\H\\to\\R:\\ x\\mapsto\\inf_{y\\in\\H}\\ f(y)+\\frac{1}{2\\gamma}\\norm{y-x}^2 .$$</p>"
},
"prox": {
"p": "03_proximity_operator.html",
"k": "def",
"t": "Proximity operator $\\prox_f$",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$ be a Hilbert space and $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$. The <strong>proximity operator</strong> of $f$ is\n$$\\prox_f:\\H\\to\\H:\\ x\\mapsto\\argmin_{y\\in\\H}\\ f(y)+\\tfrac12\\norm{y-x}^2 .$$\nFor $\\gamma\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$, $\\prox_{\\gamma f}x=\\argmin_{y}\\ f(y)+\\tfrac1{2\\gamma}\\norm{y-x}^2$ is the point where the infimum defining $\\env{\\gamma}{f}(x)$ is attained:\n$$\\env{\\gamma}{f}(x)=f(\\prox_{\\gamma f}x)+\\tfrac1{2\\gamma}\\norm{\\prox_{\\gamma f}x-x}^2 .$$</p>"
},
"prox-existence-uniqueness": {
"p": "03_proximity_operator.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Existence and uniqueness of the proximal point",
"s": "<p>Let $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$ and $\\gamma\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$. For every $x\\in\\H$ there exists a <em>unique</em> $p\\in\\H$ such that\n$$f(p)+\\frac1{2\\gamma}\\norm{p-x}^2=\\inf_{y\\in\\H}\\ f(y)+\\frac1{2\\gamma}\\norm{y-x}^2 .$$\nIn other words $\\prox_{\\gamma f}x$ is well defined and $\\env{\\gamma}{f}(x)\\in\\R$.</p>"
},
"prox-characterization": {
"p": "03_proximity_operator.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Characterization: $p=\\prox_f x \\iff x-p\\in\\partial f(p)$",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$ be a Hilbert space and $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$. For every $x\\in\\H$,\n$$p=\\prox_f x\\iff x-p\\in\\partial f(p)\\iff x\\in(\\Id+\\partial f)(p),$$\nthat is, $\\prox_f=(\\Id+\\partial f)^{-1}$ is the <em>resolvent</em> of $\\partial f$. For $\\gamma\\gt0$: $p=\\prox_{\\gamma f}x\\iff x-p\\in\\gamma\\,\\partial f(p)\\iff \\dfrac{x-p}{\\gamma}\\in\\partial f(p)$.</p>"
},
"prox-projection": {
"p": "03_proximity_operator.html",
"k": "prop",
"t": "Prox of an indicator = projection; the obtuse-angle characterization; $\\env{\\gamma}{\\ind_C}=\\tfrac1{2\\gamma}d_C^2$",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$ be a Hilbert space and $C$ a nonempty closed convex subset of $\\H$. Then for every $x\\in\\H$\n$$\\prox_{\\ind_C}(x)=\\argmin_{y\\in C}\\ \\tfrac12\\norm{y-x}^2=\\PC(x),$$\nthe projection of $x$ onto $C$. Moreover:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Variational characterization.</strong> $p=\\PC(x)\\iff x-p\\in\\partial\\ind_C(p)=\\NC(p)\\iff$ <span class=\"hl\">$p\\in C$ and $(\\forall y\\in C)\\ \\ps{y-p}{x-p}\\le0$</span>.</li>\n<li><strong>Vector space.</strong> If $C$ is a closed vector space: $p=\\PC(x)\\iff p\\in C$ and $x-p\\in C^\\perp$.</li>\n<li><strong>Envelope.</strong> $\\env{\\gamma}{\\ind_C}=(2\\gamma)^{-1}d_C^2$, where $d_C:x\\mapsto\\inf_{y\\in C}\\norm{y-x}=\\norm{x-\\PC x}$ is the distance to $C$.</li>\n</ul>"
},
"prox-power": {
"p": "03_proximity_operator.html",
"k": "prop",
"t": "Prox of $\\chi\\abs{\\cdot}^q$ for $q\\in\\{1,\\tfrac43,\\tfrac32,2,3,4\\}$",
"s": "<p>Let $\\chi\\gt0$, $q\\in[1,+\\infty[$ and $\\varphi:\\R\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]:\\xi\\mapsto\\chi\\abs{\\xi}^q$. Then for every $\\xi\\in\\R$, $\\prox_\\varphi\\xi$ is</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table class=\"cmp cook\"><thead><tr><th>$q$</th><th>$\\prox_{\\chi\\abs{\\cdot}^q}\\,\\xi$</th><th>name / shape</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>$1$</td><td>$\\sgn(\\xi)\\max\\{\\abs{\\xi}-\\chi,0\\}$</td><td>soft-thresholding: dead zone $[-\\chi,\\chi]$, then shift by $\\chi$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$\\tfrac43$</td><td>$\\xi+\\dfrac{4\\chi}{3\\cdot2^{1/3}}\\Big((\\eps-\\xi)^{1/3}-(\\eps+\\xi)^{1/3}\\Big)$, $\\ \\eps=\\sqrt{\\xi^2+256\\chi^3/729}$</td><td>cubic in $\\abs{p}^{1/3}$ (Cardano); flat near $0$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$\\tfrac32$</td><td>$\\xi+\\dfrac{9\\chi^2\\sgn(\\xi)}{8}\\Big(1-\\sqrt{1+\\dfrac{16\\abs{\\xi}}{9\\chi^2}}\\Big)$</td><td>quadratic in $\\abs{p}^{1/2}$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$2$</td><td>$\\dfrac{\\xi}{1+2\\chi}$</td><td>linear shrinkage</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$3$</td><td>$\\sgn(\\xi)\\,\\dfrac{\\sqrt{1+12\\chi\\abs{\\xi}}-1}{6\\chi}$</td><td>quadratic in $\\abs p$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$4$</td><td>$\\Big(\\dfrac{\\eps+\\xi}{8\\chi}\\Big)^{1/3}-\\Big(\\dfrac{\\eps-\\xi}{8\\chi}\\Big)^{1/3}$, $\\ \\eps=\\sqrt{\\xi^2+1/(27\\chi)}$</td><td>cubic in $p$ (Cardano); $\\approx\\xi$ near $0$, $\\sim\\abs{\\xi}^{1/3}$ far away</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p class=\"mini\">$(\\cdot)^{1/3}$ is the real cube root (odd function). All six formulas were checked numerically against the equation $p+q\\chi\\abs p^{q-1}\\sgn(p)=\\xi$.</p>"
},
"prox-quadratic": {
"p": "03_proximity_operator.html",
"k": "prop",
"t": "Prox of $\\gamma\\norm{L\\cdot-z}^2/2$",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$, $\\G$ be Hilbert spaces, $L\\in\\B(\\H,\\G)$, $\\gamma\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$ and $z\\in\\G$. If $f=\\gamma\\norm{L\\cdot-z}^2/2$, then\n$$\\prox_f=(\\Id+\\gamma L^*L)^{-1}(\\cdot+\\gamma L^*z),\\qquad\\text{i.e.}\\qquad \\prox_f x=(\\Id+\\gamma L^*L)^{-1}(x+\\gamma L^*z).$$</p>"
},
"prox-calculus": {
"p": "03_proximity_operator.html",
"k": "prop",
"t": "Prox calculus: translation, quadratic perturbation, scaling, reflection, Moreau envelope",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$ be a Hilbert space, $x\\in\\H$ and $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$.</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table class=\"cmp cook\"><thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>$g(x)$</th><th>$\\prox_g x$</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Translation</td><td>$f(x-z)$, $z\\in\\H$</td><td>$z+\\prox_f(x-z)$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Quadratic perturbation</td><td>$f(x)+\\alpha\\norm x^2/2+\\ps zx+\\gamma$, $z\\in\\H$, $\\alpha\\gt0$, $\\gamma\\in\\R$</td><td>$\\prox_{\\frac{f}{\\alpha+1}}\\Big(\\dfrac{x-z}{\\alpha+1}\\Big)$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Scaling</td><td>$f(\\rho x)$, $\\rho\\in\\R^*$</td><td>$\\dfrac1\\rho\\prox_{\\rho^2 f}(\\rho x)$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Reflection</td><td>$f(-x)$</td><td>$-\\prox_f(-x)$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Moreau envelope</td><td>$\\env{\\gamma}{f}(x)=\\inf_{y}f(y)+\\tfrac1{2\\gamma}\\norm{x-y}^2$, $\\gamma\\gt0$</td><td>$\\dfrac1{1+\\gamma}\\Big(\\gamma x+\\prox_{(1+\\gamma)f}(x)\\Big)$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>"
},
"prox-separable": {
"p": "03_proximity_operator.html",
"k": "prop",
"t": "Prox of a separable sum",
"s": "<p>For every $i\\in\\{1,\\dots,n\\}$ let $\\H_i$ be a Hilbert space and $f_i\\in\\Gz{\\H_i}$. If\n$$\\big(\\forall x=(x_1,\\dots,x_n)\\in\\H_1\\times\\dots\\times\\H_n\\big)\\qquad f(x)=\\sum_{i=1}^n f_i(x_i),$$\nthen $\\big(\\forall x\\in\\H_1\\times\\dots\\times\\H_n\\big)\\quad\\prox_f(x)=\\big(\\prox_{f_i}(x_i)\\big)_{1\\le i\\le n}$.</p>"
},
"prox-orthonormal-basis": {
"p": "03_proximity_operator.html",
"k": "prop",
"t": "Prox in an orthonormal basis; soft-thresholding for $\\lambda\\norm\\cdot_1$",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$ be a separable Hilbert space with an orthonormal basis $(b_i)_{i\\in I}$. For every $i\\in I$ let $\\varphi_i\\in\\Gz\\R$ with <span class=\"hl\">$\\varphi_i\\ge0$</span>. For every $x\\in\\H$, if\n$$f(x)=\\sum_{i\\in I}\\varphi_i\\big(\\ps x{b_i}\\big)\\qquad\\text{then}\\qquad\\prox_f(x)=\\sum_{i\\in I}\\prox_{\\varphi_i}\\big(\\ps x{b_i}\\big)\\,b_i .$$\n<em>Remark (slide 13):</em> the assumption $(\\forall i)\\ \\varphi_i\\ge0$ can be relaxed if $\\H$ is finite dimensional.</p>\n<p><em>Example (slide 14):</em> $\\H=\\R^N$, $(b_i)$ the canonical basis, $f=\\lambda\\norm\\cdot_1$ with $\\lambda\\in[0,+\\infty[$:\n$$\\big(\\forall x=(x^{(i)})_{1\\le i\\le N}\\in\\R^N\\big)\\qquad\\prox_{\\lambda\\norm\\cdot_1}(x)=\\big(\\prox_{\\lambda\\abs\\cdot}(x^{(i)})\\big)_{1\\le i\\le N}=\\big(\\sgn(x^{(i)})\\max\\{\\abs{x^{(i)}}-\\lambda,0\\}\\big)_{1\\le i\\le N}.$$</p>"
},
"moreau-decomposition": {
"p": "03_proximity_operator.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Moreau decomposition: $\\prox_{\\gamma f^*}x=x-\\gamma\\prox_{\\gamma^{-1}f}(\\gamma^{-1}x)$",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$ be a Hilbert space, $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$ and $\\gamma\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$. Then\n$$(\\forall x\\in\\H)\\qquad\\prox_{\\gamma f^*}x=x-\\gamma\\,\\prox_{\\gamma^{-1}f}(\\gamma^{-1}x).$$\nWith $\\gamma=1$: $\\prox_{f^*}=\\Id-\\prox_f$, i.e. $x=\\prox_fx+\\prox_{f^*}x$ for every $x$.</p>\n<p><em>Example (slide 15):</em> if $\\H=\\R^N$ and $f=\\tfrac1q\\norm\\cdot_q^q$ with $q\\in\\,]1,+\\infty[$, then $f^*=\\tfrac1{q^*}\\norm\\cdot_{q^*}^{q^*}$ with $\\tfrac1q+\\tfrac1{q^*}=1$, and\n$$(\\forall x\\in\\R^N)\\qquad\\prox_{\\frac\\gamma{q^*}\\norm\\cdot_{q^*}^{q^*}}x=x-\\gamma\\,\\prox_{\\frac1{\\gamma q}\\norm\\cdot_q^q}(\\gamma^{-1}x).$$</p>"
},
"prox-support-function": {
"p": "03_proximity_operator.html",
"k": "prop",
"t": "$\\prox_{\\sigma_C}=\\Id-\\PC$ and soft-thresholding on an interval",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$ be a Hilbert space and $C\\subset\\H$ nonempty closed convex. Then\n$$(\\forall x\\in\\H)\\qquad\\prox_{\\sigma_C}x=x-\\PC x .$$\n<strong>Soft-thresholding.</strong> $\\H=\\R$, $\\delta_1=\\inf C$, $\\delta_2=\\sup C$. For every $x\\in\\R$,\n$$\\sigma_C(x)=\\begin{cases}\\delta_1x&\\text{if }x\\lt0\\\\0&\\text{if }x=0\\\\\\delta_2x&\\text{if }x\\gt0\\end{cases}\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\\prox_{\\sigma_C}(x)=\\operatorname{soft}_C(x)=\\begin{cases}x-\\delta_1&\\text{if }x\\lt\\delta_1\\\\0&\\text{if }x\\in C\\\\x-\\delta_2&\\text{if }x\\gt\\delta_2 .\\end{cases}$$</p>"
},
"prox-composition-semiorthogonal": {
"p": "03_proximity_operator.html",
"k": "prop",
"t": "Prox of $f\\circ L$ when $LL^*=\\mu\\Id$",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$ and $\\G$ be Hilbert spaces, $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$ and $L\\in\\B(\\G,\\H)$ such that $LL^*=\\mu\\Id$ with $\\mu\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$. Then\n$$\\prox_{f\\circ L}=\\Id-\\mu^{-1}L^*\\circ(\\Id-\\prox_{\\mu f})\\circ L .$$</p>"
},
"ex-prox-plots": {
"p": "03_proximity_operator.html",
"k": "ex",
"t": "Plot the proximity operators of $\\chi\\abs\\cdot^q$ on the same figure",
"s": "<p>Plot the graphs of the six proximity operators of the <a class=\"xref\" data-id=\"prox-power\" href=\"#prox-power\">power card</a> ($q\\in\\{1,\\tfrac43,\\tfrac32,2,3,4\\}$, same $\\chi$) on the same figure, and describe what you see.</p>"
},
"ex-prox-quadratic": {
"p": "03_proximity_operator.html",
"k": "ex",
"t": "Prove the prox formula of $\\gamma\\norm{L\\cdot-z}^2/2$",
"s": "<p>Let $L\\in\\B(\\H,\\G)$, $\\gamma\\gt0$, $z\\in\\G$ and $f=\\gamma\\norm{L\\cdot-z}^2/2$. Prove that $\\prox_f=(\\Id+\\gamma L^*L)^{-1}(\\cdot+\\gamma L^*z)$.</p>"
},
"picard": {
"p": "04_fixed_point_forward_backward.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Picard's fixed point theorem (strict contractions)",
"s": "<p>Let $T:\\H\\to\\H$ be such that</p>\n<ul>\n<li>$\\hat x\\in\\H$ is a fixed point of $T$, i.e. $\\hat x=T\\hat x$;</li>\n<li>$T$ is a <span class=\"hl\">strict contraction</span>: there exists $\\rho\\in[0,1[$ such that $\\big(\\forall (x,x')\\in\\H^2\\big)\\ \\norm{Tx-Tx'}\\le\\rho\\norm{x-x'}$.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Then the sequence defined by $x_0\\in\\H$ and $(\\forall n\\in\\N)\\ x_{n+1}=Tx_n$ converges to $\\hat x$, and $\\norm{x_n-\\hat x}\\le\\rho^n\\norm{x_0-\\hat x}$ (linear convergence).</p>"
},
"splitting-goal": {
"p": "04_fixed_point_forward_backward.html",
"k": "rem",
"t": "What we want: weaker operators, varying parameters, compositions (splitting)",
"s": "<p>Extend Picard's theorem to operators that are</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>not necessarily strictly contractive</strong> (only nonexpansive, or \"averaged\");</li>\n<li><strong>possibly dependent on the iteration number $n$</strong> (varying step sizes $\\gamma_n$, relaxation parameters $\\lambda_n$);</li>\n<li><strong>built from compositions of simpler operators</strong> — the <em>splitting</em> idea: $T=\\prox_{\\gamma f}\\circ(\\Id-\\gamma\\nabla g)$ handles $f$ and $g$ separately, each with the tool that suits it;</li>\n</ul>\n<p>and then apply this to minimization: <em>how do we relate $T$ to the objective $f$?</em> Answer: through Fermat's rule and the resolvent, $\\Argmin f=\\zer\\partial f=\\Fix\\prox_{\\gamma f}$ (<a class=\"chip t\" href=\"09_toolbox.html#T15\"><b>T15</b></a>).</p>"
},
"convergence-recall": {
"p": "04_fixed_point_forward_backward.html",
"k": "def",
"t": "Strong and weak convergence (recall)",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$ be a Hilbert space, $(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ a sequence in $\\H$ and $\\hat x\\in\\H$.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>$(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ <strong>converges strongly</strong> to $\\hat x$ if $\\lim_{n\\to+\\infty}\\norm{x_n-\\hat x}=0$. Notation: $x_n\\to\\hat x$.</li>\n<li>$(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ <strong>converges weakly</strong> to $\\hat x$ if $(\\forall y\\in\\H)\\ \\lim_{n\\to+\\infty}\\ps{y}{x_n-\\hat x}=0$. Notation: $x_n\\wto\\hat x$.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Remark.</em> In a finite-dimensional Hilbert space, strong and weak convergence are equivalent.</p>"
},
"weak-convergence-criterion": {
"p": "04_fixed_point_forward_backward.html",
"k": "prop",
"t": "Weak convergence ⇔ bounded with at most one weak sequential cluster point",
"s": "<p>Let $(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ be a sequence in $\\H$. Then $(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ converges weakly if and only if</p>\n<ul>\n<li>$(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ is <span class=\"hl\">bounded</span>, and</li>\n<li>$(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ possesses <span class=\"hl\">at most one sequential cluster point in the weak topology</span>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>($\\hat x$ is a <em>sequential cluster point in the weak topology</em> — a <em>weak cluster point</em> for short — if some subsequence $(x_{n_k})_{k\\in\\N}$ converges weakly to $\\hat x$.)</p>\n<p><em>Illustration (slide 7).</em> $x_n=(-1)^n$ in $\\R$: bounded, but two cluster points $-1$ and $1$, hence no convergence.</p>"
},
"lemma-opial": {
"p": "04_fixed_point_forward_backward.html",
"k": "lemma",
"t": "Converging distances + cluster points in $D$ ⇒ weak convergence to a point of $D$ (Opial's lemma)",
"s": "<p>Let $D$ be a nonempty subset of $\\H$ and $(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ a sequence in $\\H$. Then $(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ converges weakly to a point in $D$ if</p>\n<ul>\n<li>for every $x\\in D$, $\\big(\\norm{x_n-x}\\big)_{n\\in\\N}$ converges, and</li>\n<li>every weak sequential cluster point of $(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ lies in $D$.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"tag tag-extra\">Extra</span> The slides call it Lemma 1; in the literature it is <em>Opial's lemma</em> (1967).</p>"
},
"fejer-monotone": {
"p": "04_fixed_point_forward_backward.html",
"k": "def",
"t": "Fejér-monotone sequences and their two properties",
"s": "<p>Let $D$ be a nonempty subset of a Hilbert space $\\H$ and $(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ a sequence in $\\H$. $(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ is <span class=\"hl\">Fejér-monotone with respect to $D$</span> if\n$$(\\forall x\\in D)(\\forall n\\in\\N)\\qquad\\norm{x_{n+1}-x}\\le\\norm{x_n-x}.$$</p>\n<p><strong>Properties.</strong> If $(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ is Fejér-monotone with respect to $D$ then</p>\n<ul>\n<li>for every $x\\in D$, $\\big(\\norm{x_n-x}\\big)_{n\\in\\N}$ converges;</li>\n<li>$(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ is bounded.</li>\n</ul>"
},
"fejer-convergence": {
"p": "04_fixed_point_forward_backward.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Fejér-monotone convergence: Fejér w.r.t. $D$ + weak cluster points in $D$ ⇒ $x_n\\wto$ a point of $D$",
"s": "<p>Let $D$ be a nonempty subset of a Hilbert space $\\H$ and $(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ a sequence in $\\H$. Then $(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ converges weakly to a point in $D$ if</p>\n<ul>\n<li>$(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ is Fejér-monotone with respect to $D$, and</li>\n<li>every weak sequential cluster point of $(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ lies in $D$.</li>\n</ul>"
},
"lemma-weakly-closed": {
"p": "04_fixed_point_forward_backward.html",
"k": "lemma",
"t": "Closed convex sets are weakly sequentially closed",
"s": "<p>Let $C$ be a nonempty closed convex subset of $\\H$. If $(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ is a sequence in $C$ that converges weakly to $\\hat x$, then $\\hat x\\in C$.</p>"
},
"fix-zer": {
"p": "04_fixed_point_forward_backward.html",
"k": "def",
"t": "Fixed points $\\Fix T$ and zeros $\\zer S$",
"s": "<p>Let $C$ be a nonempty subset of a Hilbert space $\\H$.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>For $T:C\\to\\H$, the set of <strong>fixed points</strong> of $T$ is $\\Fix T=\\{x\\in C\\mid x=Tx\\}$.</li>\n<li>For a set-valued $S:C\\to2^{\\H}$, the set of <strong>zeros</strong> of $S$ is $\\zer S=\\{x\\in C\\mid 0\\in Sx\\}$.</li>\n</ul>"
},
"nonexpansive": {
"p": "04_fixed_point_forward_backward.html",
"k": "def",
"t": "Nonexpansive operator",
"s": "<p>Let $C\\subset\\H$ be a nonempty set and $T:C\\to\\H$. $T$ is <span class=\"hl\">nonexpansive</span> if\n$$\\big(\\forall(x,y)\\in C^2\\big)\\qquad\\norm{Tx-Ty}\\le\\norm{x-y}.$$</p>"
},
"demiclosedness": {
"p": "04_fixed_point_forward_backward.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Demiclosedness principle",
"s": "<p>Let $C$ be a nonempty closed convex subset of a Hilbert space $\\H$ and $T:C\\to\\H$ a nonexpansive operator. If $(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ is a sequence in $C$ that converges weakly to $\\hat x$ and if $Tx_n-x_n\\to0$, then $\\hat x\\in\\Fix T$.</p>"
},
"nonexpansive-iteration": {
"p": "04_fixed_point_forward_backward.html",
"k": "prop",
"t": "Picard iteration of a nonexpansive map converges weakly when the residual vanishes",
"s": "<p>Let $C$ be a nonempty closed convex subset of a Hilbert space $\\H$ and $T:C\\to C$ a nonexpansive operator such that $\\Fix T\\neq\\emptyset$. Let $x_0\\in C$ and $(\\forall n\\in\\N)\\ x_{n+1}=Tx_n$. If $x_n-Tx_n\\to0$, then $(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ converges weakly to a point in $\\Fix T$.</p>\n<p><em>Exercise (slide 13):</em> prove this result by showing that $(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ is Fejér-monotone with respect to $\\Fix T$. Solution in <a class=\"xref\" data-id=\"ex-nonexpansive-iteration\" href=\"#ex-nonexpansive-iteration\">exercise 1</a> below.</p>"
},
"krasnoselskii-mann": {
"p": "04_fixed_point_forward_backward.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Krasnosel'skii–Mann algorithm",
"s": "<p>Let $C$ be a nonempty closed convex subset of a Hilbert space $\\H$ and $T:C\\to C$ a nonexpansive operator such that $\\Fix T\\neq\\emptyset$. Let $(\\lambda_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ be a sequence in $[0,1]$ such that\n$$\\sum_{n\\in\\N}\\lambda_n(1-\\lambda_n)=+\\infty.$$\nLet $x_0\\in C$ and $(\\forall n\\in\\N)\\ x_{n+1}=x_n+\\lambda_n(Tx_n-x_n)$. Then</p>\n<ul>\n<li>$(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ is Fejér-monotone with respect to $\\Fix T$;</li>\n<li>$(Tx_n-x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ converges strongly to $0$;</li>\n<li>$(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ converges weakly to a point in $\\Fix T$.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Typical choice:</em> $(\\forall n\\in\\N)\\ \\lambda_n=\\lambda\\in\\,]0,1[$.</p>"
},
"averaged": {
"p": "04_fixed_point_forward_backward.html",
"k": "def",
"t": "$\\alpha$-averaged operators; the inequality characterization; firmly nonexpansive",
"s": "<p>Let $C\\subset\\H$ be a nonempty set, $A:C\\to\\H$ and $\\alpha\\in\\,]0,1[$. $A$ is an <span class=\"hl\">$\\alpha$-averaged</span> operator if there exists a nonexpansive operator $R:C\\to\\H$ such that\n$$A=(1-\\alpha)\\Id+\\alpha R.$$</p>\n<p><strong>Characterization.</strong> $A$ is $\\alpha$-averaged if and only if\n$$\\big(\\forall(x,y)\\in C^2\\big)\\qquad\\norm{Ax-Ay}^2+\\frac{1-\\alpha}{\\alpha}\\norm{(\\Id-A)x-(\\Id-A)y}^2\\le\\norm{x-y}^2.$$</p>\n<p><em>Remark.</em> When $\\alpha=\\tfrac12$, we say that $A$ is <span class=\"hl\">firmly nonexpansive</span>: $\\norm{Ax-Ay}^2+\\norm{(\\Id-A)x-(\\Id-A)y}^2\\le\\norm{x-y}^2$, equivalently $\\ps{Ax-Ay}{x-y}\\ge\\norm{Ax-Ay}^2$.</p>"
},
"averaged-calculus": {
"p": "04_fixed_point_forward_backward.html",
"k": "prop",
"t": "Convex combinations and compositions of averaged operators",
"s": "<p>Let $C\\subset\\H$ be a nonempty set, $A_1:C\\to\\H$ be $\\alpha_1$-averaged and $A_2:C\\to\\H$ be $\\alpha_2$-averaged, with $(\\alpha_1,\\alpha_2)\\in\\,]0,1[^2$.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Convex combination.</strong> Let $\\lambda\\in\\,]0,1[$. $\\lambda A_1+(1-\\lambda)A_2$ is $\\gamma$-averaged with $$\\gamma=\\lambda\\alpha_1+(1-\\lambda)\\alpha_2.$$</li>\n<li><strong>Composition.</strong> Assume that $A_2(C)\\subset C$. Then $A_1\\circ A_2$ is $\\gamma$-averaged with\n$$\\gamma=\\frac{\\alpha_1+\\alpha_2-2\\alpha_1\\alpha_2}{1-\\alpha_1\\alpha_2}.$$</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Exercise (slide 17):</em> prove the first property. Solution in <a class=\"xref\" data-id=\"ex-averaged-combination\" href=\"#ex-averaged-combination\">exercise 2</a>.</p>"
},
"descent-lemma": {
"p": "04_fixed_point_forward_backward.html",
"k": "lemma",
"t": "Descent lemma: a $\\nu$-Lipschitz gradient gives a quadratic upper bound",
"s": "<p>Let $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$ be differentiable with a $\\nu$-Lipschitzian gradient ($\\nu\\gt0$): $(\\forall x,y)\\ \\norm{\\nabla f(x)-\\nabla f(y)}\\le\\nu\\norm{x-y}$. Then\n$$\\big(\\forall(x,y)\\in\\H^2\\big)\\qquad f(y)\\le f(x)+\\ps{y-x}{\\nabla f(x)}+\\frac\\nu2\\norm{y-x}^2.$$</p>"
},
"baillon-haddad": {
"p": "04_fixed_point_forward_backward.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Baillon–Haddad: $\\Id-\\nabla f$ is $\\nu/2$-averaged (co-coercivity of the gradient)",
"s": "<p>Let $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$ and $\\nu\\in\\,]0,2[$. If $f$ is differentiable with a $\\nu$-Lipschitzian gradient then $\\Id-\\nabla f$ is a $\\nu/2$-averaged operator.</p>\n<p>Along the way (and valid for every $\\nu\\gt0$) one proves the <span class=\"hl\">Baillon–Haddad inequality</span> (co-coercivity):\n$$\\big(\\forall(x,y)\\in\\H^2\\big)\\qquad\\ps{y-x}{\\nabla f(y)-\\nabla f(x)}\\ \\ge\\ \\frac1\\nu\\norm{\\nabla f(y)-\\nabla f(x)}^2.$$</p>"
},
"gradient-step-averaged": {
"p": "04_fixed_point_forward_backward.html",
"k": "cor",
"t": "$\\Id-\\gamma\\nabla f$ is $\\gamma\\nu/2$-averaged for $\\gamma\\in\\,]0,2/\\nu[$ (the gradient descent operator)",
"s": "<p>Let $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$, $\\nu\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$ and $\\gamma\\in\\,]0,2/\\nu[$. If $f$ is differentiable with a $\\nu$-Lipschitzian gradient then $\\Id-\\gamma\\nabla f$ is a $\\gamma\\nu/2$-averaged operator.</p>\n<p><em>Remark.</em> $\\Id-\\gamma\\nabla f$ is the gradient descent operator.</p>"
},
"prox-firmly-nonexpansive": {
"p": "04_fixed_point_forward_backward.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "$\\prox_f$ is firmly nonexpansive (i.e. $\\tfrac12$-averaged)",
"s": "<p>Let $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$. Then $\\prox_f$ is firmly nonexpansive, i.e. a $\\tfrac12$-averaged operator:\n$$\\big(\\forall(u_1,u_2)\\in\\H^2\\big)\\qquad\\norm{u_1-u_2}^2\\ \\ge\\ \\norm{\\prox_fu_1-\\prox_fu_2}^2+\\norm{(\\Id-\\prox_f)u_1-(\\Id-\\prox_f)u_2}^2,$$\nequivalently $\\ps{\\prox_fu_1-\\prox_fu_2}{u_1-u_2}\\ge\\norm{\\prox_fu_1-\\prox_fu_2}^2$. In particular $\\prox_f$ is nonexpansive, and so is $\\prox_{\\gamma f}$ for every $\\gamma\\gt0$.</p>"
},
"averaged-iteration": {
"p": "04_fixed_point_forward_backward.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Relaxed iteration of an $\\alpha$-averaged operator ($\\lambda_n\\in[0,1/\\alpha]$)",
"s": "<p>Let $T:\\H\\to\\H$ be an $\\alpha$-averaged operator with $\\alpha\\in\\,]0,1[$ such that $\\Fix T\\neq\\emptyset$. Let $(\\lambda_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ be a sequence in $[0,1/\\alpha]$ such that\n$$\\sum_{n\\in\\N}\\lambda_n(1-\\alpha\\lambda_n)=+\\infty.$$\nLet $x_0\\in\\H$ and $(\\forall n\\in\\N)\\ x_{n+1}=x_n+\\lambda_n(Tx_n-x_n)$. Then</p>\n<ul>\n<li>$(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ is Fejér-monotone with respect to $\\Fix T$;</li>\n<li>$(Tx_n-x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ converges strongly to $0$;</li>\n<li>$(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ converges weakly to a point in $\\Fix T$.</li>\n</ul>"
},
"forward-backward": {
"p": "04_fixed_point_forward_backward.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Forward–Backward algorithm",
"s": "<p>Let $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$. Let $g\\in\\Gz{\\H}$ be differentiable with a $\\nu$-Lipschitzian gradient, $\\nu\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$. Let $\\gamma\\in\\,]0,2/\\nu[$ and $\\delta=2-\\gamma\\nu/2\\in\\,]1,2[$. Let $(\\lambda_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ be a sequence in $[0,\\delta]$ such that $\\sum_{n\\in\\N}\\lambda_n(\\delta-\\lambda_n)=+\\infty$. We assume that $\\Argmin(f+g)\\neq\\emptyset$. Let $x_0\\in\\H$ and\n$$(\\forall n\\in\\N)\\qquad\\begin{cases}y_n=x_n-\\gamma\\nabla g(x_n)\\\\ x_{n+1}=x_n+\\lambda_n\\big(\\prox_{\\gamma f}y_n-x_n\\big).\\end{cases}$$\nThen $(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ converges weakly to a minimizer of $f+g$.</p>"
},
"forward-backward-varying": {
"p": "04_fixed_point_forward_backward.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Forward–Backward with varying stepsize",
"s": "<p>Let $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$. Let $g\\in\\Gz{\\H}$ be differentiable with a $\\nu$-Lipschitzian gradient, $\\nu\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$. Let $(\\gamma_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ be a sequence in $[\\underline\\gamma,\\overline\\gamma]$ where $0\\lt\\underline\\gamma\\lt\\overline\\gamma\\lt2/\\nu$, and let $(\\lambda_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ be a sequence in $[\\underline\\lambda,1]$ with $0\\lt\\underline\\lambda\\le1$. We assume that $\\Argmin(f+g)\\neq\\emptyset$. Let $x_0\\in\\H$ and\n$$(\\forall n\\in\\N)\\qquad\\begin{cases}y_n=x_n-\\gamma_n\\nabla g(x_n)\\\\ x_{n+1}=x_n+\\lambda_n\\big(\\prox_{\\gamma_nf}y_n-x_n\\big).\\end{cases}$$\nThen $(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ converges weakly to a minimizer of $f+g$.</p>"
},
"projected-gradient": {
"p": "04_fixed_point_forward_backward.html",
"k": "cor",
"t": "Projected gradient algorithm ($f=\\ind_C$)",
"s": "<p>Let $C$ be a nonempty closed convex subset of $\\H$. Let $g\\in\\Gz{\\H}$ be differentiable with a $\\nu$-Lipschitzian gradient, $\\nu\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$. Let $\\gamma\\in\\,]0,2/\\nu[$ and $\\delta=2-\\gamma\\nu/2\\in\\,]1,2[$. Let $(\\lambda_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ be a sequence in $[0,\\delta]$ such that $\\sum_{n\\in\\N}\\lambda_n(\\delta-\\lambda_n)=+\\infty$. We assume that $\\Argmin_{x\\in C}g(x)\\neq\\emptyset$. Let $x_0\\in\\H$ and\n$$(\\forall n\\in\\N)\\qquad\\begin{cases}y_n=x_n-\\gamma\\nabla g(x_n)\\\\ x_{n+1}=x_n+\\lambda_n\\big(P_Cy_n-x_n\\big).\\end{cases}$$\nThen $(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ converges weakly to a minimizer of $g$ over $C$.</p>"
},
"gradient-descent": {
"p": "04_fixed_point_forward_backward.html",
"k": "cor",
"t": "Gradient descent ($f=0$)",
"s": "<p>Let $g\\in\\Gz{\\H}$ be a differentiable function with a $\\nu$-Lipschitzian gradient, $\\nu\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$. Let $(\\gamma_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ be a sequence in $[\\underline\\gamma,\\overline\\gamma]$ where $0\\lt\\underline\\gamma\\lt\\overline\\gamma\\lt2/\\nu$. We assume that $\\Argmin g\\neq\\emptyset$. Let $x_0\\in\\H$ and\n$$(\\forall n\\in\\N)\\qquad x_{n+1}=x_n-\\gamma_n\\nabla g(x_n).$$\nThen $(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ converges weakly to a minimizer of $g$.</p>"
},
"proximal-point": {
"p": "04_fixed_point_forward_backward.html",
"k": "cor",
"t": "Proximal point algorithm ($g=0$)",
"s": "<p>Let $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$. Let $(\\gamma_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ be a sequence in $]0,+\\infty[$ such that $\\sum_{n\\in\\N}\\gamma_n=+\\infty$. We assume that $\\Argmin f\\neq\\emptyset$. Let $x_0\\in\\H$ and\n$$(\\forall n\\in\\N)\\qquad x_{n+1}=\\prox_{\\gamma_nf}x_n.$$\nThen $(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ converges weakly to a minimizer of $f$.</p>"
},
"dr-motivation": {
"p": "05_douglas_rachford_ppxa.html",
"k": "rem",
"t": "Which algorithm needs what: a splitting algorithm for two nonsmooth functions",
"s": "<p>Let $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$ and $g\\in\\Gz{\\H}$. We want to $$\\operatorname*{minimize}_{x\\in\\H}\\ f(x)+g(x).$$ Possible solutions and what they require:</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Algorithm</th><th>Needs</th><th>Iteration</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><a class=\"xref\" data-id=\"gradient-descent\" href=\"04_fixed_point_forward_backward.html#gradient-descent\">gradient descent</a></td><td><span class=\"ko\">$f+g$ smooth</span> (Lipschitz gradient)</td><td>$x_{n+1}=x_n-\\gamma\\nabla(f+g)(x_n)$</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a class=\"xref\" data-id=\"proximal-point\" href=\"04_fixed_point_forward_backward.html#proximal-point\">proximal point</a></td><td><span class=\"ko\">$f+g$ \"proximable\"</span> (one prox for the sum)</td><td>$x_{n+1}=\\prox_{\\gamma_n(f+g)}x_n$</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a class=\"xref\" data-id=\"forward-backward\" href=\"04_fixed_point_forward_backward.html#forward-backward\">Forward–Backward</a></td><td><span class=\"mid\">$g$ smooth</span>, $f$ proximable</td><td>$x_{n+1}=x_n+\\lambda_n\\big(\\prox_{\\gamma f}(x_n-\\gamma\\nabla g(x_n))-x_n\\big)$</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Douglas–Rachford</strong> (this page)</td><td><span class=\"ok\">$f$ and $g$ each proximable</span>, nothing smooth</td><td>$y_n=\\prox_{\\gamma g}x_n$, $z_n=\\prox_{\\gamma f}(2y_n-x_n)$, $x_{n+1}=x_n+\\lambda_n(z_n-y_n)$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Can we find a splitting algorithm when both $f$ and $g$ are nonsmooth?</strong> Splitting means: the algorithm only ever calls $\\prox_{\\gamma f}$ and $\\prox_{\\gamma g}$ separately, never a prox of the sum.</p>"
},
"rprox-nonexpansive": {
"p": "05_douglas_rachford_ppxa.html",
"k": "prop",
"t": "The reflected prox $\\rprox_{\\gamma f}=2\\prox_{\\gamma f}-\\Id$ is nonexpansive",
"s": "<p>Let $\\gamma\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$ and let $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$. The <strong>reflection of the proximity operator</strong>, defined as $$\\rprox_{\\gamma f}=2\\prox_{\\gamma f}-\\Id,$$ is nonexpansive: $(\\forall x,y\\in\\H)\\ \\norm{\\rprox_{\\gamma f}x-\\rprox_{\\gamma f}y}\\le\\norm{x-y}$.</p>"
},
"ex-rprox": {
"p": "05_douglas_rachford_ppxa.html",
"k": "ex",
"t": "Establish that $\\rprox_{\\gamma f}$ is nonexpansive",
"s": "<p>Let $\\gamma\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$ and $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$. Establish that $\\rprox_{\\gamma f}=2\\prox_{\\gamma f}-\\Id$ is nonexpansive.</p>"
},
"dr-fixed-points": {
"p": "05_douglas_rachford_ppxa.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Solutions are shadows of fixed points: $\\zer(\\partial f+\\partial g)=\\prox_{\\gamma g}(\\Fix T)$, $T=\\rprox_{\\gamma f}\\circ\\rprox_{\\gamma g}$",
"s": "<p>Let $\\gamma\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$, let $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$ and let $g\\in\\Gz{\\H}$. We have $$\\zer(\\partial f+\\partial g)=\\prox_{\\gamma g}(\\Fix T)\\qquad\\text{where }T=\\rprox_{\\gamma f}\\circ\\rprox_{\\gamma g}.$$ In words: $x$ is a zero of $\\partial f+\\partial g$ if and only if $x=\\prox_{\\gamma g}y$ for some fixed point $y$ of $T$.</p>"
},
"douglas-rachford": {
"p": "05_douglas_rachford_ppxa.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Douglas–Rachford algorithm and its convergence",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$ be a Hilbert space. Let $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$ and $g\\in\\Gz{\\H}$. Let $\\gamma\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$ and let $(\\lambda_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ be a sequence in $[0,2]$ such that $\\sum_{n\\in\\N}\\lambda_n(2-\\lambda_n)=+\\infty$. We assume that $\\zer(\\partial f+\\partial g)\\neq\\emptyset$. Let $x_0\\in\\H$ and</p>\n<div class=\"algo-box\"><div class=\"algo-name\">Douglas–Rachford</div>\n<p>For $n=0,1,\\dots$</p>\n<ol><li>$y_n=\\prox_{\\gamma g}x_n$ <span class=\"mini\">(the shadow: the candidate solution)</span></li>\n<li>$z_n=\\prox_{\\gamma f}(2y_n-x_n)$ <span class=\"mini\">(prox of $f$ at the reflection of $x_n$ through $y_n$)</span></li>\n<li>$x_{n+1}=x_n+\\lambda_n(z_n-y_n)$ <span class=\"mini\">(relaxed update of the governing variable)</span></li></ol></div>\n<p>The following properties are satisfied:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>$x_n\\wto\\hat x$;</li>\n<li>$\\hat y=\\prox_{\\gamma g}\\hat x\\in\\Argmin(f+g)$;</li>\n<li>$z_n-y_n\\to0$;</li>\n<li>$y_n\\wto\\hat y$ and $z_n\\wto\\hat y$.</li>\n</ol>"
},
"peaceman-rachford": {
"p": "05_douglas_rachford_ppxa.html",
"k": "rem",
"t": "Peaceman–Rachford: the limit case $\\lambda_n\\equiv2$",
"s": "<p>The limit case when $(\\forall n\\in\\N)\\ \\lambda_n=2$ is the <strong>Peaceman–Rachford algorithm</strong>. Its convergence is only guaranteed under some additional assumption (e.g. strong convexity of $g$).</p>"
},
"weak-continuity-bounded": {
"p": "05_douglas_rachford_ppxa.html",
"k": "lemma",
"t": "Bounded linear operators are weakly continuous",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$ and $\\G$ be two Hilbert spaces and let $L\\in\\B(\\G,\\H)$. Then $L$ is weakly continuous: for every sequence $(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ of $\\G$, $$x_n\\wto x\\ \\Rightarrow\\ Lx_n\\wto Lx .$$</p>"
},
"parallel-dr": {
"p": "05_douglas_rachford_ppxa.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Parallel form of Douglas–Rachford for $g\\circ L$",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$ and $\\G$ be two Hilbert spaces. Let $g\\in\\Gz{\\H}$ and $L\\in\\B(\\G,\\H)$ be such that $\\ran L$ is closed and $L^*L$ is an isomorphism. Let $\\gamma\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$ and let $(\\lambda_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ be a sequence in $[0,2]$ such that $\\sum_{n\\in\\N}\\lambda_n(2-\\lambda_n)=+\\infty$. Assume that $\\zer(L^*\\circ\\partial g\\circ L)\\neq\\emptyset$. Let $x_0\\in\\H$, $v_0=(L^*L)^{-1}L^*x_0$ and</p>\n<div class=\"algo-box\"><div class=\"algo-name\">Parallel Douglas–Rachford</div>\n<p>For $n=0,1,\\dots$</p>\n<ol><li>$y_n=\\prox_{\\gamma g}x_n$ <span class=\"mini\">(prox in the big space $\\H$)</span></li>\n<li>$c_n=(L^*L)^{-1}L^*y_n$ <span class=\"mini\">(least squares: $Lc_n=P_Ey_n$, $E=\\ran L$)</span></li>\n<li>$x_{n+1}=x_n+\\lambda_n\\big(L(2c_n-v_n)-y_n\\big)$ <span class=\"mini\">($L(2c_n-v_n)=P_E(2y_n-x_n)=z_n$)</span></li>\n<li>$v_{n+1}=v_n+\\lambda_n(c_n-v_n)$ <span class=\"mini\">(the same update read in $\\G$)</span></li></ol></div>\n<p>Then $v_n\\wto\\hat v$ where $\\hat v\\in\\Argmin(g\\circ L)$. (The proof also gives $c_n\\wto\\hat v$, as stated on slide 8.)</p>"
},
"ppxa": {
"p": "05_douglas_rachford_ppxa.html",
"k": "algo",
"t": "PPXA+: parallel proximal algorithm for $\\sum_{i=1}^m g_i\\circ L_i$",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H_1,\\dots,\\H_m$ and $\\G$ be Hilbert spaces. For every $i\\in\\{1,\\dots,m\\}$, let $g_i\\in\\Gz{\\H_i}$ and let $L_i\\in\\B(\\G,\\H_i)$. Assume that $\\sum_{i=1}^mL_i^*L_i$ is an isomorphism. Let $\\gamma\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$ and let $(\\lambda_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ be a sequence in $[0,2]$ such that $\\sum_{n\\in\\N}\\lambda_n(2-\\lambda_n)=+\\infty$. Assume that $\\zer\\big(\\sum_{i=1}^mL_i^*\\circ\\partial g_i\\circ L_i\\big)\\neq\\emptyset$. Let $(x_{0,i})_{1\\le i\\le m}\\in\\H_1\\times\\dots\\times\\H_m$, $v_0=\\big(\\sum_{i=1}^mL_i^*L_i\\big)^{-1}\\sum_{i=1}^mL_i^*x_{0,i}$ and</p>\n<div class=\"algo-box\"><div class=\"algo-name\">PPXA+</div>\n<p>For $n=0,1,\\dots$</p>\n<ol><li>$y_{n,i}=\\prox_{\\gamma g_i}(x_{n,i}),\\quad i\\in\\{1,\\dots,m\\}$ <span class=\"mini\">(<strong>parallel</strong>: $m$ independent proxes)</span></li>\n<li>$c_n=\\big(\\sum_{i=1}^mL_i^*L_i\\big)^{-1}\\sum_{i=1}^mL_i^*y_{n,i}$ <span class=\"mini\">(<strong>synchronization</strong>: gather, one linear solve)</span></li>\n<li>$x_{n+1,i}=x_{n,i}+\\lambda_n\\big(L_i(2c_n-v_n)-y_{n,i}\\big),\\quad i\\in\\{1,\\dots,m\\}$ <span class=\"mini\">(<strong>parallel</strong>: broadcast $2c_n-v_n$, local update)</span></li>\n<li>$v_{n+1}=v_n+\\lambda_n(c_n-v_n)$ <span class=\"mini\">(the small variable)</span></li></ol></div>\n<p>Then $v_n\\wto\\hat v\\in\\Argmin\\sum_{i=1}^mg_i\\circ L_i$.</p>"
},
"ppxa-consensus": {
"p": "05_douglas_rachford_ppxa.html",
"k": "cor",
"t": "Consensus case $L_i=\\Id$: the parallel proximal algorithm (PPXA)",
"s": "<p>Let $g_1,\\dots,g_m\\in\\Gz{\\G}$ with $\\zer\\big(\\sum_{i=1}^m\\partial g_i\\big)\\neq\\emptyset$, $\\gamma\\gt0$, $(\\lambda_n)$ in $[0,2]$ with $\\sum\\lambda_n(2-\\lambda_n)=+\\infty$. Let $(x_{0,i})_{1\\le i\\le m}\\in\\G^m$, $v_0=\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^mx_{0,i}$ and</p>\n<div class=\"algo-box\"><div class=\"algo-name\">PPXA (consensus form)</div>\n<p>For $n=0,1,\\dots$</p>\n<ol><li>$y_{n,i}=\\prox_{\\gamma g_i}(x_{n,i}),\\quad i\\in\\{1,\\dots,m\\}$ <span class=\"mini\">(parallel)</span></li>\n<li>$c_n=\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^my_{n,i}$ <span class=\"mini\">(the average)</span></li>\n<li>$x_{n+1,i}=x_{n,i}+\\lambda_n(2c_n-v_n-y_{n,i}),\\quad i\\in\\{1,\\dots,m\\}$ <span class=\"mini\">(parallel)</span></li>\n<li>$v_{n+1}=v_n+\\lambda_n(c_n-v_n)$</li></ol></div>\n<p>Then $v_n\\wto\\hat v\\in\\Argmin\\sum_{i=1}^mg_i$.</p>"
},
"ex-parallel-algorithms": {
"p": "05_douglas_rachford_ppxa.html",
"k": "ex",
"t": "Parallel algorithms for $\\sum g_i$, for projections + support functions, and for $\\norm{\\cdot}_1$ over an intersection",
"s": "<p>Let $\\G$ be a real Hilbert space. Let $m\\in\\N$, $m\\ge2$.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>For every $i\\in\\{1,\\dots,m\\}$, let $g_i$ be a function in $\\Gz{\\G}$ such that $\\dom g_1\\cap\\bigcap_{i=2}^m\\inte(\\dom g_i)\\neq\\emptyset$. Propose a parallel algorithm for finding a minimizer of $\\sum_{i=1}^mg_i$ when such a minimizer exists.</li>\n<li>Let $p\\in\\{1,\\dots,m-1\\}$ and let $(C_i)_{1\\le i\\le m}$ be nonempty closed convex subsets of $\\G$. We assume that $\\bigcap_{i=1}^p\\inte(C_i)\\neq\\emptyset$, and for every $j\\in\\{p+1,\\dots,m\\}$, $C_j$ is bounded. Propose a parallel algorithm for solving the problem $$\\operatorname*{minimize}_{x\\in\\bigcap_{i=1}^pC_i}\\ \\sum_{j=p+1}^m\\sigma_{C_j}(x)$$ when this problem admits a solution.</li>\n<li>Let $\\H=\\R^N$. Deduce an algorithm to $$\\operatorname*{minimize}_{x\\in\\bigcap_{i=1}^pC_i}\\ \\norm{x}_1 .$$</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"mini\">Part V version: item 1 without the qualification condition, item 2 without \"$C_j$ bounded\", no item 3.</p>"
},
"primal-dual-problems": {
"p": "06_duality_admm_primal_dual.html",
"k": "def",
"t": "Primal and dual problems",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$ and $\\G$ be two real Hilbert spaces, $f:\\H\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$, $g:\\G\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$ and $L\\in\\B(\\H,\\G)$. The <strong>primal problem</strong> is\n$$\\underset{x\\in\\H}{\\text{minimize}}\\ f(x)+g(Lx),$$\nand the <strong>dual problem</strong> is\n$$\\underset{v\\in\\G}{\\text{minimize}}\\ f^*(-L^*v)+g^*(v).$$</p>"
},
"weak-duality": {
"p": "06_duality_admm_primal_dual.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Weak duality: $\\mu\\ge-\\mu^*$, and the duality gap",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$, $\\G$ be real Hilbert spaces, $f$ a proper function from $\\H$ to $]-\\infty,+\\infty]$, $g$ a proper function from $\\G$ to $]-\\infty,+\\infty]$, and $L\\in\\B(\\H,\\G)$. Let\n$$\\mu=\\inf_{x\\in\\H} f(x)+g(Lx)\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\\mu^*=\\inf_{v\\in\\G} f^*(-L^*v)+g^*(v).$$\nThen $\\mu\\ge-\\mu^*$. If $\\mu\\in\\R$, the number $\\mu+\\mu^*\\ (\\ge0)$ is called the <strong>duality gap</strong>.</p>"
},
"strong-duality": {
"p": "06_duality_admm_primal_dual.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Strong duality under a qualification condition",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$, $\\G$ be real Hilbert spaces, $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$, $g\\in\\Gz{\\G}$ and $L\\in\\B(\\H,\\G)$. If\n$$\\inte(\\dom g)\\cap L(\\dom f)\\ne\\emptyset\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad\\dom g\\cap\\inte\\big(L(\\dom f)\\big)\\ne\\emptyset,$$\nthen\n$$\\mu=\\inf_{x\\in\\H} f(x)+g(Lx)=-\\min_{v\\in\\G} f^*(-L^*v)+g^*(v)=-\\mu^*.$$\nIn particular the dual problem has a solution and the duality gap is $0$.</p>"
},
"lp-duality": {
"p": "06_duality_admm_primal_dual.html",
"k": "prop",
"t": "Example 1: linear programming duality",
"s": "<p>Let $L\\in\\R^{K\\times N}$, $b\\in\\R^K$ and $c\\in\\R^N$. The primal problem\n$$\\text{Primal-LP}:\\qquad\\underset{x\\in[0,+\\infty[^N}{\\text{minimize}}\\ \\ps{c}{x}\\quad\\text{s.t.}\\quad Lx\\ge b$$\nis associated with the dual problem\n$$\\text{Dual-LP}:\\qquad\\underset{y\\in[0,+\\infty[^K}{\\text{maximize}}\\ \\ps{b}{y}\\quad\\text{s.t.}\\quad L^{\\top}y\\le c.$$\nIn addition, if the primal problem has a solution, then strong duality holds.</p>"
},
"consensus-sharing": {
"p": "06_duality_admm_primal_dual.html",
"k": "prop",
"t": "Example 2: sharing is the dual of consensus",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$ be a real Hilbert space. For every $i\\in\\{1,\\dots,m\\}$, let $g_i:\\H\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$ and $h_i:\\H\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$. The <strong>consensus problem</strong> is\n$$\\underset{\\substack{(x_1,\\dots,x_m)\\in\\H^m\\\\ x_1=\\dots=x_m}}{\\text{minimize}}\\ \\sum_{i=1}^m g_i(x_i).$$\nThe <strong>sharing problem</strong> is\n$$\\underset{\\substack{(u_1,\\dots,u_m)\\in\\H^m\\\\ u_1+\\dots+u_m=\\overline u}}{\\text{maximize}}\\ \\sum_{i=1}^m h_i(u_i),\\qquad\\overline u\\in\\H.$$\nIf, for every $i$, $h_i=-g_i^*(\\cdot-\\overline u/m)$, then sharing is the dual problem of consensus.</p>"
},
"sum-rule-recall": {
"p": "06_duality_admm_primal_dual.html",
"k": "rem",
"t": "The sum rule for $f+g\\circ L$ (slide 9)",
"s": "<p>Let $f:\\H\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$, $g:\\G\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$, $L\\in\\B(\\H,\\G)$. If $\\dom g\\cap L(\\dom f)\\ne\\emptyset$, then for every $x\\in\\H$, $\\partial f(x)+L^*\\partial g(Lx)\\subset\\partial(f+g\\circ L)(x)$. If moreover $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$, $g\\in\\Gz{\\G}$ and $\\inte(\\dom g)\\cap L(\\dom f)\\ne\\emptyset$ or $\\dom g\\cap\\inte(L(\\dom f))\\ne\\emptyset$, then $\\partial f+L^*\\partial gL=\\partial(f+g\\circ L)$.</p>"
},
"duality-theorem-1": {
"p": "06_duality_admm_primal_dual.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Duality theorem (1): primal zeros exist ⇔ dual zeros exist",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$, $\\G$ be real Hilbert spaces, $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$, $g\\in\\Gz{\\G}$ and $L\\in\\B(\\H,\\G)$. Then\n$$\\zer\\big(\\partial f+L^*\\partial gL\\big)\\ne\\emptyset\\iff\\zer\\big((-L)\\partial f^*(-L^*\\cdot)+\\partial g^*\\big)\\ne\\emptyset.$$</p>"
},
"duality-theorem-2": {
"p": "06_duality_admm_primal_dual.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Duality theorem (2): Kuhn–Tucker points give primal and dual solutions",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$, $\\G$ be real Hilbert spaces, $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$, $g\\in\\Gz{\\G}$ and $L\\in\\B(\\H,\\G)$.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If there exists $\\hat x\\in\\H$ such that $0\\in\\partial f(\\hat x)+L^*\\partial g(L\\hat x)$, then $\\hat x$ is a solution to the primal problem. Moreover, there exists a solution $\\hat v$ to the dual problem such that $-L^*\\hat v\\in\\partial f(\\hat x)$ and $L\\hat x\\in\\partial g^*(\\hat v)$.</li>\n<li>If there exists $(\\hat x,\\hat v)\\in\\H\\times\\G$ such that $-L^*\\hat v\\in\\partial f(\\hat x)$ and $L\\hat x\\in\\partial g^*(\\hat v)$, then $\\hat x$ (resp. $\\hat v$) is a solution to the primal (resp. dual) problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A pair $(\\hat x,\\hat v)\\in\\H\\times\\G$ such that $-L^*\\hat v\\in\\partial f(\\hat x)$ and $L\\hat x\\in\\partial g^*(\\hat v)$ is called a <strong>Kuhn–Tucker point</strong>.</p>"
},
"kt-prox-form": {
"p": "06_duality_admm_primal_dual.html",
"k": "rem",
"t": "Particular case: $f=\\varphi+\\tfrac12\\norm{\\cdot-z}^2$ gives $\\hat x=\\prox_\\varphi(-L^*\\hat v+z)$",
"s": "<p>If $f=\\varphi+\\tfrac12\\norm{\\cdot-z}^2$ with $\\varphi\\in\\Gz{\\H}$ and $z\\in\\H$, then for every $(\\hat x,\\hat v)\\in\\H\\times\\G$,\n$$-L^*\\hat v\\in\\partial f(\\hat x)\\iff-L^*\\hat v\\in\\partial\\varphi(\\hat x)+\\hat x-z\\iff0\\in\\hat x+L^*\\hat v-z+\\partial\\varphi(\\hat x),$$\nhence $\\hat x=\\prox_\\varphi(-L^*\\hat v+z)$.</p>"
},
"lagrangian-minimax": {
"p": "06_duality_admm_primal_dual.html",
"k": "prop",
"t": "Minimax problem: the primal as $\\inf\\sup$ of the Lagrangian",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$, $\\G$ be real Hilbert spaces, $f:\\H\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$, $g:\\G\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$ and $L\\in\\B(\\H,\\G)$. The primal problem is equivalent to finding\n$$\\mu=\\inf_{(x,y)\\in\\H\\times\\G}\\ \\sup_{v\\in\\G}\\ \\Lag(x,y,v),$$\nwhere $\\Lag$ is the <strong>Lagrange function</strong> defined by\n$$\\big(\\forall(x,y,v)\\in\\H\\times\\G^2\\big)\\qquad\\Lag(x,y,v)=f(x)+g(y)+\\ps{v}{Lx-y}.$$</p>"
},
"lagrangian-maximin": {
"p": "06_duality_admm_primal_dual.html",
"k": "prop",
"t": "Maximin problem: the dual as $\\sup\\inf$ of the Lagrangian; Lagrange multipliers",
"s": "<p>With the same notation, the dual problem is equivalent to finding\n$$-\\mu^*=\\sup_{v\\in\\G}\\ \\inf_{(x,y)\\in\\H\\times\\G}\\ \\Lag(x,y,v),\\qquad\\Lag(x,y,v)=f(x)+g(y)+\\ps{v}{Lx-y}.$$\n<em>Remark.</em> $v$ is called the <strong>Lagrange multiplier</strong> associated with the constraint $Lx=y$.</p>"
},
"saddle-point": {
"p": "06_duality_admm_primal_dual.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Saddle points of the Lagrangian ⇔ Kuhn–Tucker points with $\\hat y=L\\hat x$",
"s": "<p><strong>Definition.</strong> $(\\hat x,\\hat y,\\hat v)\\in\\H\\times\\G^2$ is a <strong>saddle point</strong> of the Lagrange function $\\Lag$ if\n$$\\big(\\forall(x,y,v)\\in\\H\\times\\G^2\\big)\\qquad\\Lag(\\hat x,\\hat y,v)\\le\\Lag(\\hat x,\\hat y,\\hat v)\\le\\Lag(x,y,\\hat v).$$\n<strong>Theorem.</strong> Let $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$, $g\\in\\Gz{\\G}$, $L\\in\\B(\\H,\\G)$ and $(\\hat x,\\hat y,\\hat v)\\in\\H\\times\\G^2$. Assume that $\\inte(\\dom g)\\cap L(\\dom f)\\ne\\emptyset$ or $\\dom g\\cap\\inte(L(\\dom f))\\ne\\emptyset$. Then\n$$(\\hat x,\\hat y,\\hat v)\\ \\text{is a saddle point of }\\Lag\\iff(\\hat x,\\hat v)\\ \\text{is a Kuhn–Tucker point and}\\ \\hat y=L\\hat x.$$</p>"
},
"admm-idea": {
"p": "06_duality_admm_primal_dual.html",
"k": "rem",
"t": "Alternating minimization of $\\Lag$ fails in general; the augmented Lagrangian",
"s": "<p>Iterations for finding a saddle point $(\\hat x,\\hat y,\\hat v)$:\n$$(\\forall n\\in\\N)\\qquad\\begin{cases}x_n\\in\\Argmin\\Lag(\\cdot,y_n,v_n)\\\\ y_{n+1}\\in\\Argmin\\Lag(x_n,\\cdot,v_n)\\\\ v_{n+1}\\ \\text{such that}\\ \\Lag(x_n,y_{n+1},v_{n+1})\\ge\\Lag(x_n,y_{n+1},v_n).\\end{cases}$$\nBut the convergence is not guaranteed in general. <strong>Solution:</strong> introduce an <strong>augmented Lagrange function</strong>. Let $\\gamma\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$ and define\n$$\\big(\\forall(x,y,z)\\in\\H\\times\\G^2\\big)\\qquad\\widetilde{\\Lag}(x,y,z)=f(x)+g(y)+\\gamma\\ps{z}{Lx-y}+\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\norm{Lx-y}^2.$$\nThe Lagrange multiplier is $v=\\gamma z$.</p>"
},
"augmented-lagrangian-saddle": {
"p": "06_duality_admm_primal_dual.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Saddle points of the augmented Lagrangian",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$, $\\G$ be real Hilbert spaces, $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$, $g\\in\\Gz{\\G}$, $L\\in\\B(\\H,\\G)$ and $(\\hat x,\\hat y,\\hat z)\\in\\H\\times\\G^2$. Assume that $\\inte(\\dom g)\\cap L(\\dom f)\\ne\\emptyset$ or $\\dom g\\cap\\inte(L(\\dom f))\\ne\\emptyset$. Then\n$$(\\hat x,\\hat y,\\hat z)\\ \\text{is a saddle point of }\\widetilde{\\Lag}\\iff(\\hat x,\\gamma\\hat z)\\ \\text{is a Kuhn–Tucker point and}\\ \\hat y=L\\hat x.$$</p>"
},
"admm-derivation": {
"p": "06_duality_admm_primal_dual.html",
"k": "algo",
"t": "ADMM derived from the augmented Lagrangian",
"s": "<p>Algorithm for finding a saddle point of $\\widetilde{\\Lag}$:\n$$(\\forall n\\in\\N)\\qquad\\begin{cases}x_n=\\argmin_{x\\in\\H}\\widetilde{\\Lag}(x,y_n,z_n)\\\\ y_{n+1}=\\argmin_{y\\in\\G}\\widetilde{\\Lag}(x_n,y,z_n)\\\\ z_{n+1}\\ \\text{such that}\\ \\widetilde{\\Lag}(x_n,y_{n+1},z_{n+1})\\ge\\widetilde{\\Lag}(x_n,y_{n+1},z_n).\\end{cases}$$\nBy performing a gradient ascent on the Lagrange multiplier (step $1/\\gamma$),\n$$(\\forall n\\in\\N)\\qquad\\begin{cases}x_n=\\argmin_{x\\in\\H}\\ f(x)+\\gamma\\ps{z_n}{Lx-y_n}+\\frac\\gamma2\\norm{Lx-y_n}^2\\\\ y_{n+1}=\\argmin_{y\\in\\G}\\ g(y)+\\gamma\\ps{z_n}{Lx_n-y}+\\frac\\gamma2\\norm{Lx_n-y}^2\\\\ z_{n+1}=z_n+\\frac1\\gamma\\nabla_z\\widetilde{\\Lag}(x_n,y_{n+1},z_n)\\end{cases}\\iff\\begin{cases}x_n=\\argmin_{x\\in\\H}\\ \\frac12\\norm{Lx-y_n+z_n}^2+\\frac1\\gamma f(x)\\\\ y_{n+1}=\\prox_{g/\\gamma}(z_n+Lx_n)\\\\ z_{n+1}=z_n+Lx_n-y_{n+1}.\\end{cases}$$</p>"
},
"dr-unrelaxed": {
"p": "06_duality_admm_primal_dual.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Unrelaxed Douglas–Rachford (reminder)",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$ be a Hilbert space, $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$, $g\\in\\Gz{\\H}$ and $\\gamma\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$. Assume that $\\zer(\\partial f+\\partial g)\\ne\\emptyset$. Let $u_0\\in\\H$ and\n$$(\\forall n\\in\\N)\\qquad\\begin{cases}v_n=\\prox_{\\gamma g}u_n\\\\ w_n=\\prox_{\\gamma f}(2v_n-u_n)\\\\ u_{n+1}=u_n+w_n-v_n.\\end{cases}$$\nThen: 1. $u_n\\wto\\hat u$; 2. $v_n\\wto\\hat v=\\prox_{\\gamma g}\\hat u\\in\\Argmin(f+g)$.</p>"
},
"dr-dual-form": {
"p": "06_duality_admm_primal_dual.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Dual form of unrelaxed Douglas–Rachford",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$, $\\G$ be Hilbert spaces, $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$, $g\\in\\Gz{\\G}$, $L\\in\\B(\\H,\\G)$ and $\\gamma\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$. Assume that $\\zer\\big(\\partial(f^*\\circ(-L^*))+\\partial g^*\\big)\\ne\\emptyset$. Let $u_0\\in\\G$ and\n$$(\\forall n\\in\\N)\\qquad\\begin{cases}v_n=\\prox_{\\gamma g^*}u_n\\\\ w_n=\\prox_{\\gamma f^*\\circ(-L^*)}(2v_n-u_n)\\\\ u_{n+1}=u_n+w_n-v_n.\\end{cases}$$\nThen: 1. $u_n\\wto\\hat u$; 2. $v_n\\wto\\hat v=\\prox_{\\gamma g^*}\\hat u\\in\\Argmin\\big(f^*\\circ(-L^*)+g^*\\big)$.</p>"
},
"dr-admm-equivalence": {
"p": "06_duality_admm_primal_dual.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Douglas–Rachford on the dual is ADMM",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$, $\\G$ be Hilbert spaces, $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$, $g\\in\\Gz{\\G}$, $L\\in\\B(\\H,\\G)$ such that $L^*L$ is an isomorphism, and $\\gamma\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$. The dual form of the unrelaxed Douglas–Rachford algorithm\n$$(\\forall n\\in\\N)\\qquad\\begin{cases}v_n=\\prox_{\\gamma g^*}u_n\\\\ w_n=\\prox_{\\gamma f^*\\circ(-L^*)}(2v_n-u_n)\\\\ u_{n+1}=u_n+w_n-v_n\\end{cases}$$\ncan be re-expressed as\n$$(\\forall n\\in\\N)\\qquad\\begin{cases}x_n=\\argmin_{x\\in\\H}\\ \\tfrac12\\norm{Lx-y_n+z_n}^2+\\tfrac1\\gamma f(x)\\\\ s_n=Lx_n\\\\ y_{n+1}=\\prox_{g/\\gamma}(z_n+s_n)\\\\ z_{n+1}=z_n+s_n-y_{n+1}\\end{cases}$$\nby setting $y_n=\\gamma^{-1}(u_n-v_n)$ and $z_n=\\gamma^{-1}v_n$.</p>"
},
"lemma-graph-closed": {
"p": "06_duality_admm_primal_dual.html",
"k": "lemma",
"t": "The graph of $\\partial f$ is closed for strong × weak convergence",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$ be a Hilbert space and $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$. If $(x_n,v_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ is a sequence of $\\H^2$ such that $v_n\\in\\partial f(x_n)$, $x_n\\to\\hat x$ and $v_n\\wto\\hat v$, then $\\hat v\\in\\partial f(\\hat x)$.</p>"
},
"admm-convergence": {
"p": "06_duality_admm_primal_dual.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Convergence of ADMM",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$, $\\G$ be Hilbert spaces, $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$, $g\\in\\Gz{\\G}$, $L\\in\\B(\\H,\\G)$ such that $L^*L$ is an isomorphism, and $\\gamma\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$. Assume that $\\inte(\\dom g)\\cap L(\\dom f)\\ne\\emptyset$ or $\\dom g\\cap\\inte(L(\\dom f))\\ne\\emptyset$, and that $\\Argmin(f+g\\circ L)\\ne\\emptyset$. Let $(y_0,z_0)\\in\\G^2$ and\n$$(\\forall n\\in\\N)\\qquad\\begin{cases}x_n=\\argmin_{x\\in\\H}\\ \\tfrac12\\norm{Lx-y_n+z_n}^2+\\tfrac1\\gamma f(x)\\\\ s_n=Lx_n\\\\ y_{n+1}=\\prox_{g/\\gamma}(z_n+s_n)\\\\ z_{n+1}=z_n+s_n-y_{n+1}.\\end{cases}$$\nThen $x_n\\wto\\hat x$ where $\\hat x\\in\\Argmin(f+g\\circ L)$, and $\\gamma z_n\\wto\\hat v$ where $\\hat v\\in\\Argmin\\big(f^*\\circ(-L^*)+g^*\\big)$.</p>\n<p class=\"mini\">Slide 25 initializes with $u_0\\in\\G$, $v_0=\\prox_{\\gamma g^*}u_0$, $y_0=\\gamma^{-1}(u_0-v_0)$, $z_0=\\gamma^{-1}v_0$ (a genuine DR start); slide 28 states it for any $(y_0,z_0)\\in\\G^2$, which is equivalent since after one ADMM step $(y_1,z_1)$ is of that form with $u_1=\\gamma(y_1+z_1)$.</p>"
},
"pd-problem": {
"p": "06_duality_admm_primal_dual.html",
"k": "def",
"t": "The primal-dual problem $f+g\\circ L+h$ with $h$ smooth",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$, $\\G$ be real Hilbert spaces, $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$, $h\\in\\Gz{\\H}$, $g\\in\\Gz{\\G}$ and $L\\in\\B(\\H,\\G)$. It is assumed that $h$ is differentiable and has a $\\beta$-Lipschitzian gradient with $\\beta\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$. We want to\n$$\\underset{x\\in\\H}{\\text{minimize}}\\ f(x)+g(Lx)+h(x).$$</p>"
},
"admm-limitations": {
"p": "06_duality_admm_primal_dual.html",
"k": "rem",
"t": "ADMM for $f+g\\circ L+h$ and its three limitations",
"s": "<p><strong>ADMM algorithm.</strong> Let $\\gamma\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$.\n$$(\\forall n\\in\\N)\\qquad\\begin{cases}x_n=\\argmin_{x\\in\\H}\\ \\tfrac12\\norm{Lx-y_n+z_n}^2+\\tfrac1\\gamma\\big(f(x)+h(x)\\big)\\\\ y_{n+1}=\\prox_{g/\\gamma}(z_n+Lx_n)\\\\ z_{n+1}=z_n+Lx_n-y_{n+1}.\\end{cases}$$\n<strong>Limitations.</strong> (i) The computation of $x_n$ at iteration $n$ may be complicated. (ii) Convergence requires $L^*L$ to be invertible. (iii) The smoothness of $h$ is not exploited.</p>"
},
"arrow-hurwicz": {
"p": "06_duality_admm_primal_dual.html",
"k": "algo",
"t": "Idea 1: Arrow–Hurwicz, explicit primal-dual subgradient steps",
"s": "<p>The optimization problem is reformulated as finding\n$$\\inf_{x\\in\\H}\\ f(x)+h(x)+\\underbrace{\\sup_{v\\in\\G}\\ \\ps{v}{Lx}-g^*(v)}_{g(Lx)}=\\inf_{x\\in\\H}\\sup_{v\\in\\G}\\ f(x)+h(x)+\\ps{v}{Lx}-g^*(v).$$\n<strong>Arrow–Hurwicz method.</strong> Let $(\\tau_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ and $(\\sigma_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ be sequences in $]0,+\\infty[$.\n$$(\\forall n\\in\\N)\\qquad\\begin{cases}t_n\\in\\partial f(x_n)\\\\ x_{n+1}=x_n-\\tau_n\\big(t_n+\\nabla h(x_n)+L^*v_n\\big)\\\\ s_n\\in\\partial g^*(v_n)\\\\ v_{n+1}=v_n-\\sigma_n\\big(s_n-Lx_{n+1}\\big)\\end{cases}$$\n⇝ requires stringent conditions on the choice of the step-size (e.g. decaying to zero).</p>"
},
"pd-implicit": {
"p": "06_duality_admm_primal_dual.html",
"k": "algo",
"t": "Idea 2: implicit updates become prox steps",
"s": "<p>Use implicit updates (the subgradients are taken at the <em>new</em> points):\n$$(\\forall n\\in\\N)\\quad\\begin{cases}t_n\\in\\partial f(x_{n+1})\\\\ x_{n+1}=x_n-\\tau_n\\big(t_n+\\nabla h(x_n)+L^*v_n\\big)\\\\ s_n\\in\\partial g^*(v_{n+1})\\\\ v_{n+1}=v_n-\\sigma_n\\big(s_n-Lx_{n+1}\\big)\\end{cases}\\iff\\begin{cases}0\\in x_{n+1}-x_n+\\tau_n\\big(\\nabla h(x_n)+L^*v_n\\big)+\\tau_n\\partial f(x_{n+1})\\\\ 0\\in v_{n+1}-v_n-\\sigma_nLx_{n+1}+\\sigma_n\\partial g^*(v_{n+1})\\end{cases}$$\n$$\\iff\\begin{cases}x_{n+1}=\\prox_{\\tau_nf}\\big(x_n-\\tau_n(\\nabla h(x_n)+L^*v_n)\\big)\\\\ v_{n+1}=\\prox_{\\sigma_ng^*}\\big(v_n+\\sigma_nLx_{n+1}\\big)\\end{cases}$$\n⇝ still does not converge for constant values of the step-size.</p>"
},
"primal-dual-algorithm": {
"p": "06_duality_admm_primal_dual.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Idea 3: extrapolate $2x_{n+1}-x_n$ — the primal-dual algorithm and its convergence",
"s": "<p><strong>Idea 3.</strong> Use the approximation $x_{n+1}\\simeq2x_{n+1}-x_n$ in the dual step.</p>\n<p><strong>Convergence of the PD algorithm.</strong> Let $\\H$, $\\G$ be Hilbert spaces, $L\\in\\B(\\H,\\G)$, $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$, $g\\in\\Gz{\\G}$, and $h\\in\\Gz{\\H}$ with a $\\beta$-Lipschitzian gradient. Let $\\tau\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$ and $\\sigma\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$. Assume that\n$$\\tau^{-1}-\\sigma\\norm{L}^2\\gt\\beta/2\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\\zer\\big(\\partial f+\\nabla h+L^*\\partial gL\\big)\\ne\\emptyset.$$\nLet $x_0\\in\\H$, $v_0\\in\\G$, and\n$$(\\forall n\\in\\N)\\qquad\\begin{cases}x_{n+1}=\\prox_{\\tau f}\\big(x_n-\\tau(\\nabla h(x_n)+L^*v_n)\\big)\\\\ v_{n+1}=\\prox_{\\sigma g^*}\\big(v_n+\\sigma L(2x_{n+1}-x_n)\\big).\\end{cases}$$\nThen $x_n\\wto\\hat x\\in\\Argmin(f+h+g\\circ L)$ and $v_n\\wto\\hat v\\in\\Argmin\\big((f+h)^*\\circ(-L^*)+g^*\\big)$.</p>\n<p><em>Remark.</em> When $g=0$, we recover the Forward–Backward algorithm.</p>"
},
"primal-dual-relaxed": {
"p": "06_duality_admm_primal_dual.html",
"k": "thm",
"t": "Relaxed form of the primal-dual algorithm",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$, $\\G$ be Hilbert spaces, $L\\in\\B(\\H,\\G)$, $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$, $g\\in\\Gz{\\G}$, and $h\\in\\Gz{\\H}$ with a $\\beta$-Lipschitzian gradient, $\\beta\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$. Let $\\tau\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$ and $\\sigma\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$. Assume that $\\tau^{-1}-\\sigma\\norm{L}^2\\ge\\beta/2$ and $\\zer(\\partial f+\\nabla h+L^*\\partial gL)\\ne\\emptyset$. Let\n$$\\delta=2-\\frac\\beta2\\big(\\tau^{-1}-\\sigma\\norm{L}^2\\big)^{-1}\\in[1,2[,$$\nand let $(\\lambda_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ be a sequence in $[0,\\delta]$ such that $\\sum_{n\\in\\N}\\lambda_n(\\delta-\\lambda_n)=+\\infty$. Let $x_0\\in\\H$, $v_0\\in\\G$ and\n$$(\\forall n\\in\\N)\\qquad\\begin{cases}p_n=\\prox_{\\tau f}\\big(x_n-\\tau(\\nabla h(x_n)+L^*v_n)\\big)\\\\ q_n=\\prox_{\\sigma g^*}\\big(v_n+\\sigma L(2p_n-x_n)\\big)\\\\ (x_{n+1},v_{n+1})=(x_n,v_n)+\\lambda_n\\big(p_n-x_n,\\ q_n-v_n\\big).\\end{cases}$$\nThen $(x_n)_{n\\in\\N}$ converges weakly to a minimizer of $f+h+g\\circ L$.</p>"
},
"ex-consensus-sharing": {
"p": "06_duality_admm_primal_dual.html",
"k": "ex",
"t": "Prove that sharing is the dual of consensus",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$ be a real Hilbert space, $g_i,h_i:\\H\\to\\,]-\\infty,+\\infty]$ for $i\\in\\{1,\\dots,m\\}$, $\\overline u\\in\\H$. Prove that if $h_i=-g_i^*(\\cdot-\\overline u/m)$ for every $i$, then the sharing problem $\\max\\sum_ih_i(u_i)$ s.t. $u_1+\\dots+u_m=\\overline u$ is the dual of the consensus problem $\\min\\sum_ig_i(x_i)$ s.t. $x_1=\\dots=x_m$. Make the distributed-computing reading explicit: what does each agent compute, what is exchanged?</p>"
},
"ex-primal-dual-sum": {
"p": "06_duality_admm_primal_dual.html",
"k": "ex",
"t": "A primal-dual algorithm for $f+\\sum_ig_i\\circ L_i+h$",
"s": "<p>Let $\\H$ and $(\\G_i)_{1\\le i\\le m}$ be real Hilbert spaces. Let $f\\in\\Gz{\\H}$, $h\\in\\Gz{\\H}$, and, for every $i\\in\\{1,\\dots,m\\}$, $g_i\\in\\Gz{\\G_i}$ and $L_i\\in\\B(\\H,\\G_i)$. It is assumed that $h$ is differentiable and has a $\\beta$-Lipschitzian gradient with $\\beta\\in\\,]0,+\\infty[$. Propose a primal-dual algorithm to solve\n$$\\underset{x\\in\\H}{\\text{minimize}}\\ f(x)+\\sum_{i=1}^mg_i(L_ix)+h(x).$$</p>"
}
};
